var e={notice:`The data here is/may be copyrighted and NOT included in the GPLv3 license. Converted from MegaMek's unit files (https://github.com/MegaMek/megamek): MegaMek Data (C) 2025-2026 by The MegaMek Team, licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/).`,shared:[{manufacturer:`New Samarkand Metals|Luthien Armor Works`,primaryFactory:`New Samarkand|Luthien`},`The Kage ("shadow") light battle armor was one of the first Draconis Combine designs to enter production following the Truce of Tukayyid. Developed jointly by Internal Security Force scientists and Draconis Elite Strike Team advisers, the suit was built for reconnaissance, infiltration, and intelligence gathering in hostile environments. Produced by New Samarkand Metals and Luthien Armor Works, the Kage entered service in 3056 as a replacement for the older DEST Infiltration Suit, offering far greater protection and mobility. The design earned a strong reputation within DCMS special operations and line formations alike for its exceptional aerial agility and advanced stealth characteristics.`,`The DEST-specific Kage arms every trooper in the team with a small laser rather than using the squad support weapon arrangement of standard configurations. This gives the entire team consistent anti-armor firepower at the cost of reduced jump capability, with assisted jumps limited to 90 meters compared to the standard 120-meter range. The suit retains its 750-kilogram mass and 275 kilograms of basic stealth armor with full electronic and infrared suppression. Armored gloves on both hands remain free for manipulation tasks.

Equipping every trooper with a laser eliminates the vulnerability inherent in the standard configuration, where only one team member carries the heavy weapon. If that trooper is lost in a standard loadout, the team loses its primary offensive capability. The DEST variant distributes firepower evenly, ensuring the team retains combat effectiveness even after taking casualties. The reduced jump range is an accepted compromise for teams that expect to face powered opposition during infiltration missions.`,`Research and development of the Kage began in March 3051 as a joint project between Internal Security Force scientists and Draconis Elite Strike Team advisers. The program grew from a failed prototype of the Raiden battle armor, repurposing its chassis for a dedicated reconnaissance and infiltration role. First prototypes appeared in May 3052, but the design team quickly encountered a serious problem. DCMS commanders had demanded a suit capable of outjumping most BattleMechs, and the jump jet configuration necessary to achieve those distances produced dangerously unstable flight characteristics. Several test operators were killed before engineers devised a solution: a partial wing with stabilizing fins that could retract flush against the suit when not in use. This redesign consumed three and a half additional years of development before mass production finally commenced in March 3056.

The Kage entered service as a direct replacement for the DEST Infiltration Suit, and within years it had virtually displaced the older sneak suit across Combine special operations units. Where the Infiltration Suit provided stealth alone, the Kage combined infiltration capability with enough armor and mobility to survive contact with powered opponents. Regular DCMS line formations also received Kage suits, employing them primarily as forward observers and artillery spotters for Arrow IV batteries using light TAG designators.

One of the suit's earliest high-profile deployments came during Operation Serpent, the Inner Sphere's strike against the Clan Smoke Jaguar homeworld of Huntress. Three DEST teams wearing Kage suits infiltrated the planet's surface with orders to destroy the command and control center coordinating orbital defenses. The teams overcame Elemental guards at the facility and completed their mission, clearing the way for the invasion fleet to press the attack. The Kage's stealth and jump capability proved critical in allowing the teams to bypass ground-level security and approach the target undetected.

During the Jihad, Kage-equipped DEST operatives carried out another critical operation in 3069, extracting Hohiro Kurita from Word of Blake captivity. That same year saw brutal fighting on Luthien itself, where a close-combat variant armed with vibro-claws appeared for the first time. These weaponless but lethally equipped suits proved effective against unarmored targets in the close quarters of urban combat. Luthien Armor Works had begun producing this variant, but the plant was destroyed mere months after the production line opened. New Samarkand Metals continued as the primary source for all Kage production through the Jihad and into the Dark Age, adding a space operations variant designed for boarding actions and the experimental Kage C, which incorporated advanced stealth technology salvaged from captured Blakist suits along with a networked sensor system to enhance the Kage's value within coordinated formations.

The Kage remained a cornerstone of Combine special operations capability through the Republic era and beyond. Its combination of stealth, aerial agility, and compact size ensured continued demand even as newer battle armor designs entered service. DCMS doctrine continued to favor Kage teams for deep reconnaissance, target designation, and sabotage missions where heavier suits would compromise operational security.`,{la:{manipulator:`armored-glove`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`armored-glove`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},[{tag:`is-small-laser`,location:`ra`,uuid:`#0`}],{manufacturer:`New Samarkand Metals`,primaryFactory:`New Samarkand`},`The space operations Kage replaces the standard armored gloves and support weapon configuration with a battle claw and cutting torch optimized for boarding actions. Jump boosters installed in the partial wing structure provide maneuvering capability in zero-gravity environments, allowing troopers to cross gaps between vessels and navigate hull exteriors. The suit retains its 750-kilogram mass and basic stealth armor for protection, though the reconfigured wing assembly changes the suit's atmospheric flight profile.

The battle claw enables troopers to grip hull surfaces, breach access panels, and engage defenders in close quarters, while the cutting torch handles sealed hatches and structural barriers. This configuration sacrifices ranged firepower entirely in favor of breaching capability, making it dependent on surprise and speed during boarding operations. The lack of any ranged weapon beyond whatever anti-personnel sidearm the trooper carries leaves the team at a severe disadvantage if detected before reaching the target vessel.`,{la:{manipulator:`armored-glove`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-jump-booster`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-cutting-torch`,`ra`,`#1`],[`is-space-operations-adaptation`,`body`,`#2`]]},`The vibro-claw Kage replaces both armored gloves with vibro-claws, trading manipulation capability and all weapon mounts for lethal close-combat effectiveness. The suit carries no anti-personnel weapon mount and no squad support weapon, making it entirely dependent on melee range engagement. It retains the standard 750-kilogram mass, 275 kilograms of basic stealth armor with electronic and infrared suppression, and the partial wing assembly providing 120-meter assisted jumps with a retractable profile.

The vibro-claws are devastating against unarmored targets and can damage lighter battle armor suits in close quarters. Combined with the Kage's stealth and aerial mobility, troopers can close to melee range before opponents can react, striking from unexpected angles. The complete absence of ranged weaponry is a significant liability if the team is detected at distance, and the loss of armored gloves limits the troopers' ability to perform non-combat tasks such as climbing, carrying equipment, or handling prisoners.`,{la:{manipulator:`battle-claw-vibro`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`battle-claw-vibro`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},[{tag:`is-partial-wing`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`}],`The flamer configuration designates one trooper as the support element, carrying a flamer assembled from components distributed across the team via the squad support weapon mount. Remaining troopers retain anti-personnel weapon mounts on the right forearm, typically fitted with submachine guns, while all operators keep both armored gloves free for other tasks. The Kage's 750-kilogram frame carries 275 kilograms of basic stealth armor with electronic and infrared suppression capable of defeating active probes, and its partial wing assembly provides 120-meter assisted jumps with a retractable profile to preserve the suit's low radar signature.

The flamer delivers effective close-range firepower against conventional infantry and unarmored targets, making this loadout well suited for clearing defensive positions and denying terrain during infiltration operations. Its limited range forces dangerously close engagements, but the Kage's stealth and mobility typically allow troopers to close the distance before being detected.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`squadSupport`],$v:[[`is-partial-wing`,`body`,`#0`,null],[`is-flamer-ba`,`ra`,`#1`,!0]]},`The laser configuration equips one trooper with a small laser carried as a squad support weapon, with components shared across the team and assembled before engagement. The remaining troopers carry anti-personnel weapons on the right forearm, and all operators retain their armored gloves. The Kage weighs 750 kilograms and mounts 275 kilograms of basic stealth armor with integrated electronic and infrared suppression, providing protection against active sensor systems. Its partial wing assembly delivers 120-meter assisted jumps and retracts to reduce the suit's detection profile.

The small laser offers the best anti-armor capability among the standard Kage support weapon options, capable of damaging light vehicles and other battle armor suits at moderate range without dependence on ammunition. This makes the laser configuration preferable for extended operations behind enemy lines where resupply is unlikely. The weapon generates significant heat in the confined suit, however, and its energy signature can compromise stealth if fired without discipline.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`squadSupport`],$v:[[`is-partial-wing`,`body`,`#0`,null],[`is-small-laser`,`la`,`#1`,!0]]},`The machine gun configuration assigns one trooper to carry a machine gun as a squad support weapon, assembled from components distributed across the team before combat. Other troopers retain anti-personnel weapon mounts on the right forearm, and all operators keep both armored gloves free. At 750 kilograms, the Kage mounts 275 kilograms of basic stealth armor incorporating electronic and infrared suppression that defeats active probe detection. The partial wing assembly enables 120-meter assisted jumps and retracts flush against the suit to maintain a low radar cross-section.

The machine gun provides sustained automatic fire effective against infantry, light vehicles, and exposed crew. Its high rate of fire makes this configuration valuable for ambush operations and suppressive fire during extraction. The weapon's ammunition dependency limits operational endurance compared to energy weapon loadouts, and the distinctive sound signature of automatic fire can compromise the team's position during covert operations.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`squadSupport`],$v:[[`is-partial-wing`,`body`,`#0`,null],[`is-machine-gun`,`la`,`#1`,!0]]},`The TAG configuration equips one trooper with a light target acquisition gear unit carried as a squad support weapon, allowing the team to designate targets for Arrow IV artillery missiles and semi-guided munitions. The remaining troopers carry anti-personnel weapons on the right forearm, and all operators retain their armored gloves. The suit weighs 750 kilograms and carries 275 kilograms of basic stealth armor with electronic and infrared suppression capable of defeating active probe systems. Its partial wing assembly provides 120-meter assisted jumps with a retractable profile for reduced visibility.

This loadout transforms the Kage from a pure reconnaissance platform into a force multiplier, enabling precision strikes from supporting artillery batteries. DCMS line units favor this configuration for forward observation duty, where the suit's stealth allows troopers to reach and hold designation positions deep in enemy territory. The TAG provides no direct combat capability, leaving the team dependent on anti-personnel weapons and extraction speed if discovered.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`squadSupport`],$v:[[`is-partial-wing`,`body`,`#0`,null],[`is-light-tag`,`la`,`#1`,!0]]},[{location:`ra`,weapon:`inf-auto-rifle`}],`The experimental Kage C replaces the standard basic stealth armor with mimetic armor salvaged from captured Blakist Purifier suits, providing superior camouflage capability and approximately twenty percent more protection than the original design. A battle armor C3 system occupies much of the remaining weight budget, allowing the suit to share targeting data within a networked formation. The C3 system alone accounts for roughly one-third of the suit's 750-kilogram mass. Both hands retain armored gloves, but the suit carries no weapons of any kind: no anti-personnel mount and no squad support weapon.

The mimetic armor's superior concealment allows the Kage C to hold observation positions with less risk of detection, while the C3 link feeds targeting data directly to friendly units. The partial wing assembly was removed to accommodate the new systems, though standard jump jets provide 90 meters of jump capability. The complete lack of offensive armament drew criticism during field trials, and the suit's reliance on experimental salvaged technology raises questions about long-term availability of replacement parts.`,[{tag:`is-c3-system`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`}],{manufacturer:`Luthien Armor Works`,primaryFactory:`Savinsville`},`The Kanazuchi is an assault-class battle armor design produced by Luthien Armor Works for the Draconis Combine Mustered Soldiery. One of the heaviest and most heavily armored suits developed by any Inner Sphere power, its name translates from Japanese as "hammer." Introduced in 3058 after a prolonged and difficult development cycle, the Kanazuchi was designed to provide the Combine with a ground-based armored infantry platform optimized for urban defense operations. The suit's exceptional protection and interchangeable manipulator system earned it a reputation as one of the most survivable designs in service, though its lack of mobility and inability to perform anti-BattleMech attacks limited its tactical flexibility compared to lighter alternatives.`,`Introduced in 3070, this upgraded configuration addresses several shortcomings identified during the Kanazuchi's first decade of service. The right-arm medium laser gives way to a pair of support particle projection cannons split between both arms, providing more consistent ranged firepower without the severe power drain of the original weapon. Twin single-tube medium-range missile launchers with eight reloads replace the SRM-2 racks, offering greater engagement flexibility at the cost of raw salvo weight.

The heavy battle claw and dual anti-personnel mounts carry forward unchanged, preserving the suit's close-quarters capability. Armor protection remains at the same heavy level as the original, maintaining the Kanazuchi's signature survivability while delivering improved offensive output across a wider range of engagement distances.`,`Development of the Kanazuchi began in July 3053, launched immediately after the Raiden battle armor reached its prototype stage. The design team at Luthien Armor Works envisioned an assault-weight suit carrying heavier weapons and armor than any previous Combine design. Originally designated the "Komodo," the project was renamed after the popular Komodo BattleMech entered production the same year.

Progress stalled in the summer of 3054 when DCMS High Command slashed the project budget, citing a lack of confidence in the design concept. Rather than abandon the program, the development team pressed forward at their own expense, working for months without pay. They reached the prototype stage in November 3055, but initial field trials proved disastrous. The original configuration mounted an excessive array of weapons alongside a full ton of armor plating. Test pilots struggled with the suit's bulk, suffering injuries from feedback surges and torque overloads. High Command nearly cancelled the project a second time.

The design team spent the next year on a complete overhaul, stripping redundant weapons systems and refining the suit's ergonomics. When a second prototype was demonstrated before the Coordinator and the assembled High Command, its performance earned an immediate tripling of the project budget. Eighteen months of accelerated development followed, culminating in a dramatic final test. A captured Timber Wolf OmniMech fired its extended-range particle cannon directly at the suited test pilot. The blast stripped away the suit's armor plating, yet the pilot survived and managed to return fire with the main weapon. Production authorization followed within two weeks, and mass manufacture began in December 3058.

Initial output from Luthien Armor Works remained modest, but the Combine-Ghost Bear War triggered a sharp increase in demand. The DCMS prioritized urban defense deployments, and production accelerated to equip front-line formations. The Kanazuchi became a fixture of elite Combine units and a common sight across population centers, a symbol of the Dragon's commitment to protecting its subjects.

Recognition of the suit's potential prompted the DCMS to rotate a team of scientists to Luthien Armor Works testing facilities on Quentin, where they began work on an upgraded configuration. The team delivered schematics for an improved model before the Word of Blake assault on the Dieron district shattered the LAW complex on Quentin. A second production facility on Savinsville, kept secret from the public, continued manufacturing improved variants through the Jihad and into the Dark Age.

The Kanazuchi's influence extended beyond the Combine. News that an Inner Sphere power had produced an assault suit capable of surviving a direct Clan energy weapon strike alarmed the Clans. Clan Hell's Horses responded by commissioning their own assault design, and the resulting Gnome entered service as a direct counter to the Combine suit. Clan Ghost Bear later followed with the Golem for similar reasons.

By the Dark Age, the Kanazuchi showed its age against newer designs. The Zou, developed by Ishikawajima-Harima as the Kanazuchi's intended successor, entered service in 3113 with superior electronics and command capabilities. A support configuration introduced around 3120 attempted to extend the older suit's service life through modernized armor and expanded missile capacity, but the fundamental chassis limitations remained.`,{la:{manipulator:`heavy-battle-claw`,adaptor:!0,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],$v:[[`is-support-ppc`,`ra`,`#0`,null],[`is-support-ppc`,`la`,`#1`,null],[`is-mrm-1`,`body`,`#2`,4],[`is-mrm-1`,`body`,`#3`,4]]},{$k:[`location`,`weapon`],$v:[[`body`,`inf-auto-rifle`],[`body`,`inf-auto-rifle`]]},{manufacturer:`Luthien Armor Works|Luthien Armor Works`,primaryFactory:`Luthien|Savinsville`},`The standard Kanazuchi configuration mounts a medium laser in the right arm as its primary weapon, powered by twin military power packs carried in the suit's dorsal section. Two shoulder-mounted SRM-2 racks provide a single four-missile volley against hardened targets, though their firing arc is limited to a forward ninety-degree cone. Two anti-personnel weapon mounts offer flexible secondary armament, with troopers selecting from arm or torso positions depending on mission requirements. A heavy battle claw in the left arm provides close-quarters capability against light vehicles and structures.

The suit carries 750 kilograms of armor, among the heaviest protection fielded on any battle armor design. This protection comes at a cost: the Kanazuchi cannot perform swarming attacks, cannot ride OmniMech handholds, lacks jump capability entirely, and manages only 10.8 kilometers per hour on foot. These restrictions confine the suit to a ground-based urban defense role where its firepower and survivability matter more than speed.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`],$v:[[`is-medium-laser`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#2`,1,!0]]},`This configuration replaces the standard heavy battle claw with an industrial drill, converting the Kanazuchi from a pure combat platform into a dual-purpose engineering asset. The drill allows troopers to breach reinforced walls, cut through vehicle armor at contact range, and perform demolition tasks in urban environments. All other systems remain identical to the standard loadout: the right-arm medium laser, twin SRM-2 racks, anti-personnel mounts, and 750 kilograms of armor plating.

The trade-off is straightforward. The industrial drill lacks the grappling capability of the battle claw, reducing the suit's effectiveness in close combat against other battle armor, but it opens tactical options in siege warfare and fortification clearance that the standard configuration cannot match.`,{la:{manipulator:`industrial-drill`,adaptor:!0,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},`Swapping the standard heavy battle claw for a salvage arm, this configuration optimizes the Kanazuchi for battlefield recovery operations. The salvage arm allows troopers to extract components from disabled vehicles and BattleMechs, clear wreckage blocking transit routes, and perform field repairs that would otherwise require dedicated support vehicles.

Weapons and armor remain unchanged from the standard configuration: the right-arm medium laser, shoulder-mounted SRM-2 launchers, and dual anti-personnel mounts provide defensive capability while the suit's 750 kilograms of protection ensure survivability during recovery operations in contested areas. The salvage arm trades the battle claw's combat utility for logistical flexibility, making this variant valuable in the aftermath of urban engagements where heavy equipment cannot easily operate.`,{la:{manipulator:`salvage-arm`,adaptor:!0,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{manufacturer:`Grumium Creations`,primaryFactory:`Grumium`},`The Kobold is a light battle armor suit jointly developed by the Free Rasalhague Republic, ComStar, and the second Star League. Entering production in 3069 at Grumium Creations on Grumium, the design was born from the Republic’s desire for a stealth-capable scout suit after failed efforts to procure existing designs from other powers. A humanoid suit with jump capability and low-observable armor, the Kobold filled a gap in Republic and ComStar inventories for a light reconnaissance platform that could hold its own in direct combat while operating alongside heavier battle armor types.`,`The micro grenade launcher and flamer configuration pairs explosive indirect fire with close-range thermal weaponry. Each trooper carries a micro grenade launcher on the suit’s modular weapon mount, providing suppressive fire against infantry and light vehicles, while the squad leader wields a flamer to clear fortified positions and flush defenders from cover. The Kobold’s basic stealth armor can absorb a hit from a medium laser, giving it respectable protection for a light design, and its armored gloves allow troopers to make anti-BattleMech attacks and ride into battle mounted on OmniMechs. At 750 kilograms, the suit sacrificed some of the Kage’s raw speed in exchange for heavier armament, though it retains a ninety-meter jump capability that keeps it agile enough for flanking and reconnaissance work.`,`The Kobold traces its origins to 3062, when the infantry of the Free Rasalhague Republic’s Second Kavalleri sought to acquire Kage battle armor from the Draconis Combine. With tensions rising on all fronts, the Combine refused the request. Överste Jack Koslow, commander of the Second Kavalleri’s attached infantry regiments, continued lobbying his superiors until he received permission to approach ComStar and the second Star League with a proposal for a joint development program. The Republic would supply raw materials while ComStar and the SLDF provided technological expertise, with funding split equally among all three parties.

Research and development began on Grumium, where construction of a prototype manufacturing line was rushed to completion. The design team originally aimed to replicate the Kage’s capabilities but soon shifted focus toward creating a frontline combat suit that retained the Combine design’s reconnaissance strengths. Prototype testing began on schedule in 3065, with the Republic’s Second Kavalleri and ComStar’s 116th Division serving as evaluation units. The SLDF was forced to pull out of the project when ongoing conflicts among League member states cut into its funding. Early problems with the suit’s stealth systems under conditions of high humidity were identified and resolved during testing, and Grumium Creations began full-scale production in 3069.

The KungsArmé adopted the Kobold as the backbone of its battle armor battalions. The Third Drakøns, Second Freemen, Third Hussars, and Second and Fourth Kavalleri all received Kobold-equipped formations. The First Tyr was also reinforced with a battle armor regiment, though its emphasis on direct combat meant it received proportionally fewer Kobolds in favor of heavier suits. In action against Word of Blake forces, the design proved capable against Blakist Purifiers and Phalanxes, earning respect as a light suit that could hold its own in a contested battlespace.

ComStar took a different approach to Kobold deployment. Rather than concentrating the suits into large formations, individual Level Is of six troopers were distributed across Precentor Martial Davion’s reorganized armies. Elements of the First and Fourth Armies deployed on Coventry, Hesperus II, and Galatea grouped light OmniMechs with Kobold-equipped troopers into combined-arms Level IIs, using the suits as scouts and spotters for heavier assets. The deterioration of relations between ComStar and the Republic eventually severed the Com Guards’ access to Grumium’s production lines, limiting their ability to replace losses.

Improving ties between the Republic and Clan Ghost Bear provided limited access to Clan battle armor technology, though the conservative Bears remained suspicious of stealth systems and showed little interest in Inner Sphere equipment. By the mid-3070s, with the second Star League disbanded and the Republic having chosen its Ghost Bear alliance over its ComStar partnership, the Kobold had become firmly a Rasalhague design. The suit earned a rare distinction as one of the few battle armor types fielded by Inner Sphere powers, ComStar, and the Clans alike.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`squadSupport`],$v:[[`is-micro-grenade-launcher`,`ra`,`#0`,null],[`is-flamer-ba`,`la`,`#1`,!0]]},`This configuration mounts a micro grenade launcher on each trooper’s modular weapon mount alongside the squad leader’s small pulse laser support weapon. The pulse laser provides accurate energy fire at close range, making this loadout well suited for engagements where ammunition conservation matters or where the squad expects sustained contact. The micro grenade launchers retain their effectiveness against soft targets and clustered infantry. Protected by basic stealth armor rated to withstand a medium laser hit, the Kobold’s light chassis keeps its profile low while armored gloves give troopers the ability to engage BattleMechs directly or ride OmniMechs into combat. The suit’s ninety-meter jump range and 750-kilogram mass strike a balance between the Kage’s pure agility and the heavier armament expected of a frontline-capable design.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`squadSupport`],$v:[[`is-micro-grenade-launcher`,`ra`,`#0`,null],[`is-small-pulse-laser`,`la`,`#1`,!0]]},`Configured with micro grenade launchers and a light TAG unit, this loadout transforms the Kobold into a dedicated spotter formation. The squad leader’s TAG designator allows the team to guide Arrow IV artillery strikes and semi-guided munitions onto high-value targets from concealment, while the grenade launchers on the remaining troopers provide enough firepower to handle unarmored threats encountered during observation missions. Standard practice places the support weapon under the squad leader’s left arm, leaving both hands free for climbing and manipulation. The suit’s basic stealth armor and low signature work in the TAG role’s favor, helping the formation remain undetected while painting targets for heavier assets. Jump capability of ninety meters allows rapid repositioning between observation posts, and armored gloves permit anti-BattleMech attacks and OmniMech mounting when the situation demands direct engagement.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`squadSupport`],$v:[[`is-micro-grenade-launcher`,`ra`,`#0`,null],[`is-light-tag`,`la`,`#1`,!0]]},`The small laser and flamer configuration emphasizes energy-based firepower, freeing the squad from dependence on limited ammunition stores. Each trooper’s modular weapon mount carries a small laser for consistent ranged output, while the squad leader’s flamer provides close-quarters area denial. This loadout favors extended operations where resupply is uncertain, as both primary weapons draw from the suit’s power systems rather than expendable magazines. The Kobold’s basic stealth armor absorbs a medium laser hit and reduces the squad’s targeting signature at range, giving these energy-focused teams additional survivability when operating ahead of friendly lines. At 750 kilograms with a ninety-meter jump capability, the suit retains enough mobility for scouting and flanking work. Armored gloves allow anti-BattleMech attacks and OmniMech transport, keeping the formation integrated with heavier combined-arms elements.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`squadSupport`],$v:[[`is-small-laser`,`ra`,`#0`,null],[`is-flamer-ba`,`la`,`#1`,!0]]},`Pairing small lasers with a light TAG designator, this configuration balances self-defense capability with forward observation duties. The small lasers on each trooper’s modular mount give the squad credible engagement ability against light targets and battle armor, while the TAG unit allows the squad leader to designate targets for artillery and guided munitions. The combination makes this loadout effective for independent screening missions where the team may need to defend itself while performing its primary spotting role. Basic stealth armor rated against a medium laser hit and the suit’s low electromagnetic signature aid concealment during target acquisition. The Kobold’s ninety-meter jump range provides the mobility needed to reach advantageous observation positions, and armored gloves support both anti-BattleMech swarming attacks and mounted transport on OmniMechs.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`squadSupport`],$v:[[`is-small-laser`,`ra`,`#0`,null],[`is-light-tag`,`la`,`#1`,!0]]},`The Kobold IIC is a Clan-tech redesign of the original Kobold battle armor, produced by Grumium Creations for the Rasalhague Dominion. Entering service in the early 3090s, the IIC was developed to extend the Kobold platform’s operational relevance with a fundamentally different mission profile. Reconfigured as a dedicated aerial reconnaissance platform, the IIC replaced the original’s ground-combat orientation with flight capability, enhanced stealth, and hostile-environment protection, filling a scouting niche that the Dominion’s heavier Clan battle armor could not.`,`The Kobold IIC abandons the original design’s ground-combat orientation in favor of aerial reconnaissance. A VTOL movement system replaces the jump jets of the base Kobold, granting sustained flight capability that dramatically expands the suit’s operational reach as a forward observer. Improved stealth armor and an integrated ECM suite make the IIC significantly harder to detect and track than its predecessor. A light TAG designator serves as the primary mission equipment, allowing troopers to paint targets for artillery and guided munitions from concealed positions. A light machine gun provides limited self-defense capability against soft targets, while HarJel systems give the suit resilience in hostile environments including vacuum and underwater operations. The trade-off is clear: the IIC carries minimal offensive armament and relies on stealth and speed rather than firepower to survive on the battlefield.`,`The Kobold IIC emerged from a recognition within the Rasalhague Dominion that the original design had grown redundant alongside the far superior Elemental. Rather than retire the suit entirely, Grumium Creations was tasked with finding a new role that could justify continued production. The answer was to abandon the ground-based scout concept in favor of a dedicated aerial reconnaissance platform. Equipped with a VTOL movement system, improved stealth armor, and HarJel self-repair systems, the IIC traded the original’s ground-fighting orientation for unmatched flexibility as a spotter and forward observer. The redesign entered production and saw service with Dominion garrison clusters, where its ability to operate in hostile environments and avoid detection made it a valuable complement to heavier Clan battle armor in combined-arms formations.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`clan-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#0`],[`clan-light-tag`,`la`,`#1`],[`clan-light-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#2`]]},{manufacturer:`Refit - ComStar`},`The X-C3 is an experimental modification developed by ComStar as a test platform for the Battle Armor C3i networking system. Integrating the captured Blakist C3i technology required significant trade-offs: engineers removed twenty percent of the base suit’s armor and replaced both weapon mounts with a single anti-personnel weapon mount, leaving the X-C3 unable to engage anything heavier than conventional infantry on its own. The suit’s value lies entirely in its networking capability. Connected through the C3i system, a team of X-C3 troopers shares targeting data in real time, improving the accuracy of all linked friendly units in the formation. ComStar’s engineers had to develop a scratch-built operating system to interface the Blakist hardware with the Kobold’s communications suite, a process that proved more difficult than anticipated. Testing confirmed the system’s effectiveness in urban and close-quarters terrain, where short engagement ranges maximized the C3i network’s benefits.`,{$k:[`tag`],$v:[[`quirk-bad-reputation`],[`quirk-prototype`],[`quirk-improved-communications`]]},[{tag:`is-improved-c3-system`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`}],{manufacturer:`StarCorps Industries`,primaryFactory:`Emris IV`},`The Kopis is an assault-class battle armor suit developed by StarCorps Industries for the Free Worlds League. Entering service in 3077 during the final years of the Jihad, the suit was designed as a heavy urban combatant capable of sustained engagements without the ammunition constraints common to missile-armed assault suits. Built at StarCorps' Emris IV facility, the Kopis earned the nickname "The Dark Alley" for its lethality in confined urban terrain, where its heavy armament and thick armor protection more than compensated for limited ground speed. The design became a mainstay of Free Worlds League and Oriente Province battle armor formations.`,`The Kopis prioritizes sustained firepower over raw destructive potential. Two modified Martell medium lasers mounted over the shoulders serve as the suit's primary armament, backed by power packs sufficient for thirty volleys. This energy weapon approach gives troopers the ability to engage targets repeatedly without concern for ammunition supply. Twin heavy battle claws provide formidable melee capability, while anti-personnel weapon mounts on each arm allow troopers to carry secondary weapons for close-quarters work.

Advanced armor provides protection equivalent to a Clan Elemental suit, though this comes at the cost of ground speed. The Kopis is one of the slowest assault suits in production, a trade-off its designers accepted to devote more than half the suit's mass to offensive systems. Commanders typically deploy Kopis troopers via VTOL transport for offensive operations or position them as defensive anchors in urban terrain, where restricted sightlines and tight corridors negate the suit's limited mobility.`,`The Kopis emerged from a crisis in Free Worlds League battle armor production. When suspected Word of Blake operatives sabotaged Etna Foundries' Longinus factory on Oriente, Thomas Halas recognized the League's vulnerability and turned to StarCorps Industries on Emris IV. StarCorps had manufacturing infrastructure from its Gorilla industrial exoskeleton line but lacked military-grade powered armor expertise. Duchess Alys Rousset-Marik brokered a solution, leveraging her resistance network to recruit Dr. Hyram Sundahar, a scientist who had fled Blakist-controlled Irian BattleMechs. Armed with Blakist technical documents obtained through the resistance, Sundahar adapted an Irian assault suit prototype into what became the Kopis.

The first suits reached the field in late 3077, assigned to the Twelfth Atrean Dragoons under Rousset-Marik's command. They proved themselves during the liberation of Outreach, where eight Kopis suits ambushed a retreating Widowmaker company and destroyed eighty percent of the mercenary force at a cost of only three suits. The Kopis participated in the Liberation of Terra in significant numbers and became the backbone of Marik and Oriente battle armor formations in the postwar period. Oriente later leveraged its Kopis production as a diplomatic tool, trading suits to The Republic of the Sphere in exchange for access to Achileus and Phalanx designs.`,{la:{manipulator:`heavy-battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`heavy-battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-medium-laser`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-medium-laser`,`body`,`#1`]]},{$k:[`location`,`weapon`],$v:[[`ra`,`inf-auto-rifle`],[`la`,`inf-auto-rifle`]]},`Introduced as a replacement for the original Anti-Infantry configuration, the Mk II takes a fundamentally different approach to the anti-personnel mission. Two heavy grenade launchers and two heavy recoilless rifles replace the energy weapons of earlier models, giving troopers a ballistic-heavy loadout capable of suppressing infantry positions at range. Improved sensors enhance target acquisition, allowing troopers to identify threats at greater distances. The twin heavy battle claws are retained for melee engagements.

This configuration trades the energy weapon endurance of the standard Kopis for greater burst damage potential, though ammunition constraints limit sustained engagements. The heavier ballistic armament makes the Mk II more effective against light vehicles and hardened positions than the laser-armed standard model, while the improved sensors partially offset the Kopis's slow ground speed by giving troopers earlier warning of approaching threats.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-heavy-recoilless-rifle`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-heavy-recoilless-rifle`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-improved-sensors`,`la`,`#2`],[`is-heavy-grenade-launcher`,`la`,`#3`],[`is-heavy-grenade-launcher`,`ra`,`#4`]]},`A refinement of the AI Mk II, this variant downgrades the heavy recoilless rifles to medium recoilless rifles, freeing weight and volume for reactive armor plating. The reactive armor significantly improves survivability against missile and ballistic attacks, addressing one of the original Mk II's weaknesses against concentrated fire. Heavy grenade launchers and heavy battle claws carry forward from the Mk II, maintaining the configuration's ballistic-focused anti-personnel capability.

The shift to medium recoilless rifles reduces per-shot damage output but preserves the suit's ability to engage infantry and light vehicles at range. The addition of reactive armor was inspired by the successful integration of similar protection on the Xiphos battle armor, giving the Mk IIr greater staying power in prolonged engagements where sustained incoming fire would have degraded the original Mk II's combat effectiveness.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-heavy-grenade-launcher`,`la`,`#0`],[`is-heavy-grenade-launcher`,`ra`,`#1`],[`is-medium-recoilless-rifle`,`body`,`#2`],[`is-medium-recoilless-rifle`,`body`,`#3`],[`is-improved-sensors`,`body`,`#4`]]},`Developed for the final Coalition assault on Terra, this configuration replaces the standard model's twin medium lasers with a medium pulse laser and a flamer. The pulse laser offers improved accuracy against personnel targets, while the flamer excels at clearing fortified positions and flushing defenders from buildings. The heavy battle claws and anti-personnel weapon mounts are retained from the base design.

This loadout sacrifices the sustained ranged firepower of the standard model in favor of close-quarters lethality against entrenched infantry. The suit retains the same advanced armor protection and ground speed profile as other Kopis configurations, making it equally dependent on VTOL transport or defensive positioning for effective deployment. Against fanatical defenders holding urban centers, the combination of area-denial flamer and precision pulse laser proved well suited to the brutal room-by-room fighting that characterized late-Jihad clearance operations.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-medium-pulse-laser`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-flamer-ba`,`body`,`#1`]]},`Built on the AI Mk IIr chassis, the Mortar variant strips all direct-fire weaponry in favor of a pair of heavy mortars. This gives Kopis troopers an indirect fire capability rare among battle armor designs, allowing them to engage targets from behind cover or lob explosive rounds over obstacles. The reactive armor from the Mk IIr is retained, providing enhanced protection against the return fire that indirect engagements inevitably attract. Heavy battle claws serve as the suit's only close-quarters defense.

The Mortar configuration is best employed in a supporting role, delivering suppressive fire from protected positions while other units close with the enemy. The reactive armor's properties are specifically designed to counter fragmentation effects common to mortar and missile weapons, making this variant resilient against opposing indirect fire assets attempting counter-battery operations.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-heavy-mortar`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-heavy-mortar`,`body`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Gortex Multi-E`,primaryFactory:`Kittery`},`The Krise is an Inner Sphere light exoskeleton designed for civilian emergency response operations. Produced by Gortex Multi-E on Kittery, the suit entered service in the late thirty-first century as a purpose-built rescue platform intended primarily for Federated Suns emergency response teams. Carrying no weapons, the Krise was equipped with a comprehensive array of search and rescue tools. Its straightforward controls allowed operators with minimal powered armor training to employ it effectively in the field.`,`The Krise weighs four hundred kilograms and carries a comprehensive set of emergency response tools. A cutting torch mounted on the right arm handles structural breaching and debris removal, while a salvage arm on the left limb manages heavier extraction tasks. Improved sensors detect life signs through dense material, and a searchlight assists operations in low-visibility conditions. Extended life support allows the operator to work in contaminated or oxygen-deprived environments for prolonged periods.

Jump jets provide a ninety-meter jump range alongside a thirty-kilometer-per-hour ground speed, giving the suit solid mobility for reaching disaster sites. Standard armor offers adequate protection for emergency work but cannot withstand sustained heat exposure the way Clan fire-resistant plating can. Brief contact with intense flames risks permanent damage to internal systems. The Krise carries no weapons, and its design emphasis on ease of use makes it accessible to emergency personnel who lack formal powered armor training.`,`The Clan Invasion triggered rapid advances in battle armor technology across the Inner Sphere, and these breakthroughs filtered down into civilian exoskeleton development. Gortex Multi-E, the Federated Suns' leading manufacturer of emergency services equipment, applied these lighter frame designs to create a purpose-built rescue exoskeleton. Christened the Krise, the suit was designed to assist emergency response teams in locating and extracting survivors from rubble, cave-ins, and natural disasters.

The Krise proved immediately popular with civilian agencies. Simple to operate and equipped with the tools needed for common rescue scenarios, the suit filled a gap that conventional equipment could not match. Emergency response teams across the Federated Suns adopted it quickly, and production runs sold out three years in advance. The suit's standard armor offered adequate protection for its intended role, though it lacked the fire resistance found on Clan-manufactured rescue exoskeletons and remained vulnerable to concentrated heat exposure that could permanently damage internal systems.`,[{tag:`quirk-easy-to-pilot`}],{la:{manipulator:`salvage-arm`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`basic`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-cutting-torch`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-searchlight`,`la`,`#1`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#2`],[`is-improved-sensors`,`body`,`#3`]]},{manufacturer:`Etna Foundries`,primaryFactory:`Oriente`},`The Leonidas is a medium Inner Sphere battle armor suit produced by Etna Foundries on Oriente for the Duchy of Oriente's armed forces. Introduced in 3096, the design filled a gap in the Duchy's reconnaissance and infiltration capabilities caused by dwindling stocks of the Achileus stealth suit. The Leonidas prioritizes concealment and electronic warfare over raw speed or firepower, making it well suited for ambush operations, artillery spotting, and counterinsurgency patrol. A modular weapon mount allows rapid reconfiguration across multiple mission roles, from direct engagement to battlefield surveillance. The suit earned a strong reputation for tactical flexibility among operators across multiple factions.`,`The David configuration mounts a "David" Light Gauss Rifle in its modular weapon slot, providing accurate ranged firepower with fifteen rounds of ammunition. Combined with Standard Stealth armor and an Angel ECM Suite, this loadout excels at engaging targets from concealed positions where the suit's lack of jump capability is less of a liability. The Heavy Battle Claw on the left arm provides close-quarters capability against vehicles and structures, while the anti-personnel weapon mount handles unarmored threats.

At one thousand kilograms the Leonidas is classified as medium battle armor, offering reasonable protection without the bulk of heavier designs. Ground speed tops out at roughly thirty-two kilometers per hour, adequate for positional warfare but insufficient for rapid maneuver. The stealth and electronic warfare systems compensate for this mobility limitation, allowing troopers to choose engagements on favorable terms.`,`Etna Foundries on Oriente developed the Leonidas around the turn of the thirty-second century to address a critical shortage of Achileus stealth suits within the Duchy of Oriente's military. The older design had grown scarce, and Oriente's commanders needed a replacement capable of filling the same infiltration and reconnaissance roles while offering greater flexibility through modular weapon configurations.

Early operational successes drew attention from buyers across the Inner Sphere and nearby Periphery states. The Free Worlds League Military became the primary operator, with decades of exports reaching the Capellan Confederation, the Magistracy of Canopus, the Marian Hegemony, and various mercenary commands. When the League reformed, production shifted largely toward equipping Oriente's fellow provinces, though export contracts continued. Commanders typically reserved the Leonidas for more experienced and better-trained infantry soldiers.

Combat experience highlighted both the suit's strengths and limitations. During the Fourth Battle of Tamarind in 3142, a volunteer platoon used the suits to infiltrate the capital city of Zanzibar and destroy a Lyran command post in a devastating surprise assault. Native guerrillas helped the troopers evade patrols and locate the target, and the entire operation concluded before Lyran BattleMechs could respond effectively. By contrast, a Capellan infiltration force on Styk in 3133 was tracked and intercepted by Republic militia, which destroyed the entire platoon after denying the troopers the element of surprise.`,{la:{manipulator:`heavy-battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-david-light-gauss-rifle`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-angel-ecm`,`body`,`#1`]]},`The Firedrake configuration equips a Firedrake Support Needler with thirty rounds of ammunition in the modular weapon slot. The needler fires clusters of flechettes effective against infantry and lightly armored targets, providing suppressive capability in counterinsurgency and urban patrol scenarios. Standard Stealth armor and the Angel ECM Suite enable troopers to approach engagement zones undetected, while the Heavy Battle Claw on the left arm allows for close-quarters work against structures and light vehicles.

Ground movement is limited to approximately thirty-two kilometers per hour with no jump capability, restricting the suit to terrain where stealth and cover can offset its modest speed. The needler's ammunition supply is more limited than some alternative loadouts, requiring troopers to exercise fire discipline during extended operations. The anti-personnel weapon mount adds secondary firepower against unarmored threats at close range.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-firedrake-support-needler`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-angel-ecm`,`body`,`#1`]]},`The machine gun configuration loads a standard machine gun with fifty rounds of ammunition into the modular weapon slot, optimizing the suit for anti-infantry and counterinsurgency operations. The high rate of fire proves effective against unarmored personnel and light vehicles, complementing the anti-personnel weapon mount for saturation fire in close engagements. Standard Stealth armor and the Angel ECM Suite allow troopers to close with targets before opening fire, turning the suit's limited ground speed into less of a disadvantage during ambush operations.

The Heavy Battle Claw provides additional utility for breaching light structures and engaging vehicles at close range. This configuration trades the precision engagement capability of other loadouts for raw volume of fire, making it particularly suited to urban fighting and patrol suppression where multiple soft targets present themselves in quick succession. At thirty-two kilometers per hour with no jump capability, troopers must rely on concealment to close the distance before contact.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-angel-ecm`,`body`,`#1`]]},`The Sensor configuration installs an Improved Sensors package in the modular weapon slot, converting the suit into a dedicated reconnaissance platform. Combined with the Standard Stealth armor and Angel ECM Suite integrated into the base chassis, this loadout provides exceptional electronic intelligence capability for a medium battle armor suit. Troopers can detect and classify enemy forces at extended range while remaining hidden from opposing sensor sweeps.

The Heavy Battle Claw and anti-personnel weapon mount retain basic self-defense options, but this configuration is designed to gather information rather than engage in sustained combat. Ground speed remains at thirty-two kilometers per hour without jump capability, requiring operators to rely on stealth rather than speed to reach and withdraw from observation positions. The sensor package partially compensates for the suit's limited mobility, allowing troopers to monitor wide areas from fixed positions.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-improved-sensors`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-angel-ecm`,`body`,`#1`]]},`The TAG configuration replaces the modular weapon slot with a Light TAG system carrying sixty uses, transforming the suit into a dedicated artillery spotter. Paired with Standard Stealth armor and the Angel ECM Suite, this loadout creates an effective forward observation platform capable of designating targets for supporting artillery and missile systems while remaining concealed from enemy detection.

The Heavy Battle Claw and anti-personnel weapon mount provide self-defense capability, though troopers in this configuration are expected to avoid direct combat and focus on target acquisition. Ground speed of thirty-two kilometers per hour and the absence of jump capability demand careful route planning to reach observation positions. The combination of stealth technology and electronic countermeasures makes this configuration particularly difficult to locate, which is critical for troopers who must remain stationary while painting targets for indirect fire support.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-light-tag`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-angel-ecm`,`body`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Field Refit`,primaryFactory:`Various`},`The Longinus was the Free Worlds League's answer to the Clan Elemental battle armor, developed jointly with the Word of Blake beginning in 3054. Classified as a medium battle armor, the suit came closer to matching its Clan-tech inspiration than any other Inner Sphere design of the era. The FWL's alliance with the Blakists provided access to technologies that rival programs in other Great Houses lacked. First fielded in 3057, the Longinus earned a reputation as both capable and expensive, with production costs well above comparable Inner Sphere designs.`,`The "Hacked" Longinus strips down the standard suit to accommodate an experimental battle armor C³i computer system captured from the Word of Blake. To fit the electronics, jump capability is reduced to sixty meters, armor is cut by a third to 240 kilograms, and the detachable SRM launcher is removed entirely. The David configuration equips a "David" light Gauss rifle in the right-arm modular mount, providing ranged engagement capability that compensates somewhat for the suit's reduced survivability. A battle claw and anti-personnel weapon mount on the left arm are retained. The C³i system allows a squad to share targeting data with heavier networked units, extending the reach of a friendly C³ network into areas that larger platforms cannot access, such as cave complexes and fortified interiors. The substantial loss of armor and weapons payload means the "Hacked" functions more as a mobile sensor node than a frontline combatant.`,`Development of the Longinus began in October 3054 as a joint venture between the Free Worlds League and the Word of Blake. The FWLM sought to replicate the Clan Elemental battle armor, and access to Blakist technologies gave the League an advantage over rival programs in the Federated Commonwealth and Draconis Combine. The advanced nature of the design and bureaucratic friction between the League and its allies slowed progress well beyond comparable efforts elsewhere.

The first prototypes reached testing in April 3056, and the results were catastrophic. Armor composites shattered during live-fire exercises, and power systems failed at random. Investigation traced many of these failures to a hidden ComStar sympathizer embedded in the Blakist development staff. Following the operative's removal, the team produced a corrected series of prototypes in early 3057. These early suits lacked the backpack missile launcher that made the Elemental so lethal, and the FWLM demanded one be incorporated. Other Inner Sphere teams had tried and failed to combine jump capability with a shoulder-mounted launcher, producing suits that were either too heavy on the ground or too ungainly in mid-jump. The Longinus team spent eight months on the problem before devising a detachable mounting that required jettisoning the launcher before the jump jets could engage.

By this point the project had far exceeded its budget, and the League Central Coordination Commission refused further funding. Production resumed only when Captain-General Thomas Marik personally authorized additional resources and IBMU agreed to absorb some costs in exchange for access to Blakist technology. The first production suits rolled off the Irian assembly line in December 3057. They arrived too late for Operation Guerrero but saw action in the Sirian Campaign and proved their worth during Operation Bulldog, where Longinus troopers held their own against Clan Elementals.

Corean Enterprises on Stewart and Kali-Yama Weapons Industries on Kalidasa soon picked up production, making the suit a common sight throughout the FWLM. Rear-echelon formations received small allotments for security, while elite frontline units fielded company or even battalion-strength deployments. The Longinus played a considerable role in the FWLM's success in the Isle of Skye. Thomas Marik initially refused to supply the suit to the Word of Blake for their planned assault on Terra, but his stance later softened, and Longinus suits began appearing in the Word of Blake Militia in significant numbers. During the Jihad, modified variants with magnetic clamps and experimental C³i electronics expanded the suit's operational profile, though the latter project was shelved after trials revealed excessive capability trade-offs.`,{la:{manipulator:`battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-david-light-gauss-rifle`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-improved-c3-system`,`body`,`#1`]]},[{location:`la`,weapon:`inf-auto-rifle`}],`The Machine Gun variant of the "Hacked" Longinus shares the same stripped-down chassis designed to carry the experimental C³i system. Armor is reduced to 240 kilograms, jump range drops to sixty meters, and the detachable SRM launcher is eliminated. A light machine gun occupies the right-arm modular mount, offering sustained anti-infantry fire but limited effectiveness against armored targets. The battle claw and anti-personnel weapon mount on the left arm remain standard. Like the David-armed variant, the MG configuration's primary value lies in the C³i network relay capability rather than direct combat power. Troopers can feed targeting data to heavier C³-equipped units from positions that BattleMechs and vehicles cannot reach. The reduced armor makes these suits considerably more fragile than a standard Longinus squad, and trial deployments confirmed that the trade-offs were too severe for continued development.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-improved-c3-system`,`body`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Corean Enterprises|Etna Foundries|Irian BattleMechs Unlimited|Kali Yama Weapons Industries|Field Refit`,primaryFactory:`Stewart|Oriente|Irian|Kalidasa|Various`},`The Magnetic variant replaces the Longinus's signature detachable SRM-2 launcher and modular weapon mount with magnetic clamps, a standard machine gun, and a heavy machine gun. The magnetic clamps allow troopers to ride on the exterior of conventional BattleMechs and vehicles, extending operational range without requiring OmniMech transport bays. With no launcher to jettison, the suit's jump jets are available immediately, giving troopers full ninety-meter leaps from the start of an engagement. The paired machine guns deliver considerable anti-infantry firepower at short range, though the loss of the SRM capability and modular flexibility limits effectiveness against armored targets. The left arm retains its battle claw for swarming attacks and the anti-personnel weapon mount. Advanced armor protection remains unchanged from the standard Longinus, maintaining the same Clan-equivalent composite that can absorb a large laser hit.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-heavy-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-machine-gun`,`la`,`#1`],[`is-magnetic-clamps`,`body`,`#2`]]},{manufacturer:`Irian BattleMechs Unlimited|Corean Enterprises|Kali Yama Weapons Industries|Etna Foundries|Gibson Federated BattleMechs`,primaryFactory:`Irian|Stewart|Kalidasa|Oriente|Gibson`},`The Longinus carries a detachable one-shot SRM-2 on a dorsal rack that must be jettisoned before the suit's jump jets can engage, allowing leaps of up to ninety meters. The David configuration, fielded beginning in 3063, equips a "David" light Gauss rifle in the right-arm modular mount. This weapon gives the suit meaningful engagement range beyond what the flamer, laser, or machine gun options provide, allowing troopers to engage targets accurately at distances where return fire from infantry weapons is less effective. The left arm retains the standard battle claw for swarming attacks and an anti-personnel mount for close-range threats. Advanced armor composites give the Longinus protection comparable to Clan battle armor, absorbing a direct large laser hit. The combination of ranged firepower and heavy protection makes this configuration effective in open terrain where other loadouts would need to close the distance.`,{la:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`detachable`],$v:[[`is-david-light-gauss-rifle`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,!0]]},`The Longinus carries a detachable one-shot SRM-2 launcher on a specialized dorsal mounting, providing an opening salvo capability before close engagement. Internal safety mechanisms prevent the suit's jump jets from engaging until the launcher is expended and jettisoned, after which troopers can leap up to ninety meters. The Flamer configuration mounts a small flamer in the right-arm modular weapon system, well-suited for clearing infantry positions and flushing defenders from enclosed spaces. A battle claw on the left arm enables anti-BattleMech swarming attacks, supplemented by an anti-personnel weapon mount for engaging unarmored infantry. The suit's advanced armor composite provides protection roughly equivalent to Clan battle armor plating, with enough coverage to absorb a direct large laser hit. This protection comes at significant manufacturing cost, as the specialized composites require dedicated production processes.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`detachable`],$v:[[`is-flamer-ba`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,!0]]},`The Longinus mounts a detachable one-shot SRM-2 on a dorsal rack, giving troopers an opening volley before the launcher is jettisoned to enable jump jet use. Once airborne, the suit can cover ninety meters per leap. The Laser configuration equips a small laser in the right-arm modular mount, offering reliable energy-based firepower with no ammunition dependency. This makes the configuration popular for extended operations where resupply is uncertain. The left arm carries a battle claw for anti-BattleMech attacks and an anti-personnel weapon mount for dealing with unarmored threats. Advanced armor composites protect the trooper against hits up to and including a large laser, closely matching Clan-grade protection levels. The weight and bulk of this armor limit the suit's ground speed, but the jump capability offsets this restriction in most tactical situations.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`detachable`],$v:[[`is-small-laser`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,!0]]},`Troopers wearing the Longinus carry a one-shot SRM-2 on a detachable dorsal mounting. Internal safety interlocks prevent jump jet activation until the launcher is jettisoned, after which the suit can leap ninety meters. The Machine Gun configuration fits a light machine gun in the right-arm modular mount, providing sustained automatic fire effective against infantry formations and light vehicles. Ammunition supply limits endurance compared to energy-armed variants, but the volume of fire compensates in short engagements. A battle claw on the left arm supports anti-BattleMech swarming operations, while an anti-personnel weapon mount handles unarmored targets. The suit's advanced armor composite absorbs hits equivalent to a large laser strike, protection that closely mirrors Clan battle armor. Ground mobility is limited, but jump capability provides the tactical flexibility needed to close with heavier targets.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`detachable`],$v:[[`is-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,!0]]},{manufacturer:`Marian Arms, Inc.|Pinard Protectorates Limited`,primaryFactory:`Horatius|Macleod's Land`},`The Marauder is a heavy battle armor suit developed by Marian Arms, Incorporated for the Marian Hegemony Armed Forces. Introduced in 3110 as the Hegemony's second indigenous battle armor design, the Marauder takes a markedly different approach from its predecessor. Where the Ravager emphasized survivability, the Marauder prioritizes offensive output and fire support capability. The suit takes its name from the iconic Marauder BattleMech, and its weapon layout pays deliberate homage to that venerable design. The Marauder has earned a solid reputation among Periphery military forces and the mercenary commands they employ.`,`Weighing 1,500 kilograms, the Marauder is built around delivering punishing firepower at the expense of both speed and protection. Each arm mounts a Support PPC paired with an Anti-Personnel Weapon Mount, while a Light Recoilless Rifle sits on the body. A Light TAG unit rounds out the loadout, allowing troopers to designate targets for Arrow IV missiles and semi-guided long-range munitions.

Ground speed is limited to just 11 km/h, making the Marauder one of the slower suits in active service. Magnetic clamps partially offset this limitation by allowing troopers to ride friendly vehicles or BattleMechs into the fight. Armor protection sits at roughly half that of the Ravager, a conscious trade-off that has drawn criticism from field commanders. The open chassis design does make the suit easy to service, earning it high marks from maintenance crews who appreciate the accessibility of internal systems.`,`The Marauder grew out of the Marian Hegemony's expanding battle armor program, which began with Dr. Dafyyd Rou's development of the Ravager on Horatius in the mid-3080s. Building on lessons learned from their first suit, Marian Arms engineers pursued a lighter design that prioritized offensive capability over raw durability. The result entered service in 3110, drawing its name and weapon configuration from the iconic Marauder BattleMech.

The suit saw its first major combat deployment during the Landfall rebellion of 3117, when Free Leaguer dissidents seized large portions of the planet. II Legio deployed Marauder squads during the bloody street fighting in New Erewhon, where the suits proved decisive against rebel BattleMechs. The capture of the rebel leader during that engagement was broadcast across the Hegemony, bringing the Marauder to widespread public attention.

Marian Arms licensed the design to Pinard Protectorates Limited in the Taurian Concordat, and both nations sell the suit freely to mercenaries in their employ.`,{$k:[`tag`,`equipmentUuid`],$v:[[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`,null],[`quirk-accurate-weapon`,`#0`]]},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-support-ppc`,`ra`,`#1`],[`is-support-ppc`,`la`,`#2`],[`is-light-recoilless-rifle`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-light-tag`,`body`,`#3`],[`is-magnetic-clamps`,`body`,`#4`]]},{manufacturer:`Gibson Federated BattleMechs`,primaryFactory:`Gibson`},`The Demon series of battle armor emerged from Gibson Federated BattleMechs during the Jihad as the companion line to the Word of Blake's Celestial OmniMechs. Conceived by Precentor Vapula, the Manei Domini's engineering chief, the six Demon designs draw their names from the malevolent figures of various world mythologies. Each suit was crafted to fill a specific combat role while projecting an element of psychological terror against the Word's enemies. Fielded almost exclusively by the cybernetically enhanced Manei Domini elite, the Demons share the same unsettling visual aesthetic as their larger Celestial counterparts and were rarely encountered outside the Shadow Divisions.`,`The capture team configuration makes aggressive use of detachable weapon technology, mounting a battle armor taser in the right arm alongside a two-tube LRM launcher in a detachable pack. A heavy machine gun resides in the right modular mount for conventional engagements. The detachable pack's bulk restricts mobility until jettisoned. The taser can disable enemy vehicles and force BattleMech shutdowns through electromagnetic overload, though the risk of feedback injury to the operator is considerable. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor provides protection and concealment during the approach phase. This is one of the most specialized Nephilim configurations, designed to seize enemy equipment and personnel intact for interrogation or reverse engineering. A fully staffed squad can potentially disable multiple enemy units in rapid succession.`,`Alongside its counterpart the Shedu, the Nephilim rounded out the Demon series with its entry into the assault class. Debuting last in the entire line, the Nephilim's first recorded appearance came during fighting on Gibson in mid-3071. Even at that early stage, the suit's powerful build, thick sensor-defeating armor, and range of equipment configurations quickly identified it as perhaps the pinnacle of Vapula's creations.

The Nephilim was the Demon that came closest to fulfilling Vapula's original vision for the entire program. Stealthy, mobile, and well armored, its generous use of modular and detachable weapons emphasized a flexibility that the other Demons only dabbled in. Where the Tengu had been the experimental proof of concept for Celestial-like adaptability in battle armor, the Nephilim was the refined production model. Six basic configurations were identified over the course of the Jihad, and the suit's modular nature spawned an even wider variety of one-off field variants observed in engagements across the Inner Sphere. Opposing commanders learned to tell their troops only that they could expect missile or rocket fire from suits shrouded in heavy sensor-defeating armor, because predicting the specific loadout of any given Nephilim formation was essentially impossible.

Even the suit's name reinforced the connection to the Celestials. Taken from Christian legends of half-angels who lived among humanity, the designation hinted at the Nephilim's closer relation to the Celestial OmniMechs than any other Demon design, and likely at the engineering goals Vapula had aimed for when developing the entire series. If the Celestials were the angels of the Word's arsenal, the Nephilim was the bridge between those divine machines and the infernal battlesuits that fought at their feet.

Despite its capabilities, the Nephilim had one of the lowest distribution rates in the entire Demon series. As the most recently introduced design, production time had been limited, and the suit never appeared in the ranks of the regular Word of Blake Militia or Protectorate Militia. Even the Shadow Divisions lacked widespread access. Allied intelligence placed the largest confirmed concentrations in the Forty-fourth, Forty-seventh, Forty-ninth, and Fifty-second Shadow Divisions only. Commanders who faced the Nephilim in battle quickly discovered that the suits were too formidable for most other battle armor to contend with. Medium-weight combat vehicles and BattleMechs were considered the most effective means of countering these assault-class Demons.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`dwp`],$v:[[`is-heavy-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-lrm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,null],[`is-taser`,`body`,`#2`,null,null,!0]]},`The "Gauss Nephilim" replaces the standard configuration's missile-and-plasma combination with three ballistic weapons for precise, sustained anti-armor fire. A "David" light Gauss rifle in the right arm provides the primary punch, while two Magshot Gauss rifles over the shoulders deliver supplementary kinetic damage. This loadout sacrifices the burst damage of the standard model's SRM volleys for more consistent accuracy and armor penetration across multiple engagements. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor and enhanced ground speed are unchanged. The all-Gauss weapons suite eliminates concerns about heat buildup, allowing the trooper to maintain a steady rate of fire. The precision of this configuration made it particularly valued by the Manei Domini elite, whose cybernetic enhancements could fully exploit the weapons' accuracy potential.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-david-light-gauss-rifle`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-magshot-gauss-rifle`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-magshot-gauss-rifle`,`body`,`#2`]]},`The Narc variant carries a two-shot light Narc launcher in a detachable mount and a support PPC in a detachable weapon pack, supplemented by a twin-tube rocket launcher. The detachable systems impose mobility penalties until dropped. The Narc beacon allows the trooper to tag enemy units for guided munitions from allied forces, combining the spotter role with direct firepower from the support PPC. A Magshot Gauss rifle in a modular turret mount adds precision ballistic capability. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor is retained. This configuration bridges the gap between the dedicated C3-equipped spotting role and direct combat, providing the squad with both target designation capability and enough personal firepower to defend itself if the engagement turns unfavorable.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`dwp`,`shots`],$v:[[`is-support-ppc`,`body`,`#0`,!0,null],[`is-rocket-launcher-2`,`body`,`#1`,null,null],[`is-compact-narc`,`body`,`#2`,null,2]]},`The standard Nephilim focuses its firepower around a man-portable plasma rifle in a modular right-arm mount, supplemented by twin SRM-2 launchers over the shoulders. This combination provides both sustained energy fire and a missile barrage capable of threatening armored vehicles and BattleMechs. The suit's 850 kilograms of mimetic armor can absorb two large laser hits while actively distorting the trooper's visual signature to interfere with enemy targeting. Enhanced ground speed compensates for the lack of jump capability. As an assault-class design massing a full two tons, the Nephilim is too heavy for OmniMech transport and cannot conduct swarming attacks. Its role is that of a ground-based heavy weapons platform, relying on armor and firepower to dominate engagements rather than mobility and stealth.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`],$v:[[`is-plasma-rifle-man-portable`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#2`,1,!0]]},`Dubbed the "Seeker," this configuration transforms the Nephilim into a dedicated hunter-killer platform. A machine gun in the right modular mount provides anti-infantry capability, while an SRM-3 launcher with two reloads delivers sustained anti-armor fire over multiple engagements. Improved sensors and a searchlight mounted in a drum over the left shoulder enhance target detection and tracking in all conditions. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor and enhanced ground speed are retained. The combination of enhanced sensors and sensor-defeating armor creates a trooper who can detect enemies while remaining difficult to detect in return. The Seeker is optimized for tracking and eliminating specific high-value targets rather than general combat, making it a preferred configuration for the Domini's more surgically minded operatives.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],$v:[[`is-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`,null],[`is-srm-3`,`body`,`#1`,3],[`is-improved-sensors`,`body`,`#2`,null],[`is-searchlight`,`body`,`#3`,null]]},`The support variant forgoes modular mounts entirely, instead carrying a fixed small laser in the right arm and two detachable four-tube rocket launchers. This gives the Nephilim a powerful opening salvo followed by indefinite energy-based firepower once the rockets are expended. The fixed mounting reduces configuration flexibility but simplifies the suit's internal systems. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor and enhanced ground speed are unchanged. Once the rocket launchers are jettisoned, the suit becomes lighter and faster, able to press the engagement with its small laser at closer range. The combination of expendable ordnance and a reliable energy weapon makes this a practical combat loadout that requires less logistical support than the SRM-dependent Seeker variant.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-small-laser`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-rocket-launcher-4`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-rocket-launcher-4`,`body`,`#2`]]},{manufacturer:`Krupp Armament Works|Comguard Tukayyid Battle Armor Production Facility|Free Worlds Defence Industries|Arc-Royal MechWorks|Niops Project Workshop III`,primaryFactory:`Terra|Tukayyid|Gibson|Arc-Royal|Niops V`},`The Nighthawk Mk. XXI PA(L) is the progenitor of modern battle armor, developed by the Terran Hegemony from commercial exoskeleton technology through one of the most challenging military engineering programs of the Star League era. Commissioned in 2720, the Nighthawk was designed as a light power armor suit for reconnaissance and covert operations. The suit entered service exclusively with the SLDF Special Forces Command, where it equipped elite special operations teams whose very existence remained classified. Classified as Power Armor (Light), the Nighthawk provided its operators with capabilities far beyond conventional infantry while maintaining the stealth profile necessary for behind-the-lines work, establishing the conceptual foundation for every battle armor design that followed.`,`The Mk. XXI weighs four hundred kilograms and mounts Standard Stealth armor incorporating both infrared suppression and electronic countermeasures. An integral ECM suite further enhances the trooper's ability to avoid detection, making the Nighthawk exceptionally difficult to locate through conventional sensor sweeps. Jump jets allow leaps of up to ninety meters, providing excellent mobility across broken terrain and urban environments. The suit carries no integral weapons, relying instead on a pair of armored gloves that allow troopers to wield standard infantry arms, typically the Mauser 960 assault system, without loss of dexterity. Extended life support enables prolonged independent operations behind enemy lines.

The Nighthawk's protection is adequate against small arms fire but offers little resistance to crew-served weapons or BattleMech-scale armaments, limiting the suit's effectiveness to roles where stealth and evasion take priority over direct engagement.`,`The Nighthawk program grew from First Star League efforts to militarize commercial exoskeletons that had been in civilian use since the early space age. First Lord Jonathan Cameron's Terran Hegemony funded an ambitious development effort, though the technology proved stubbornly difficult to adapt for combat. Fifteen years of engineering work and twenty failed prototypes preceded the breakthrough that produced the Mk. XXI in 2718. After two years of field testing, the suit entered full production at Krupp Armament Works on Terra and was issued to the Special Forces Command in 2720.

The SLDF deployed Nighthawks exclusively through its Special Armed Services, the covert operators known as the Blackhearts. The deployment was never publicly acknowledged, and the suit's existence remained classified throughout its Star League service life. The men and women of the SAS operated with such skill that the other Star League Member States heard only vague rumors of some advanced sneak suit. Rumors persist that the Thirteenth Royal Infantry Division, stationed deep in the Draconis Combine, was among the first formations to field-test the Nighthawk, conducting secret exercises against another Royal infantry division somewhere in the Draconis Rift. Plans for a combat-oriented variant, the Mk. XXII, were drawn up for distribution to SLDF Royal Divisions. A working prototype reached completion in 2749, but the Amaris Coup and the collapse of the Star League prevented that version from entering standard production.

When the Succession Wars erupted, both variants were thought lost. The Nighthawk research, however, accompanied the SLDF on the Exodus, becoming the seed from which the Clans would eventually develop true battle armor. On Terra, Jerome Blake's newly formed ComStar uncovered a small number of surviving suits and used them to equip commando units following the creation of the Com Guards. ComStar lacked the manufacturing capability to produce replacements, and attempts to reverse-engineer the design yielded only the inferior Tornado PA(L).

The Gray Death Legion recovered several Nighthawk suits on Karbala during the later Succession Wars. Most were turned over to the New Avalon Institute of Science for study, providing Davion engineers with critical data on power armor technology. New production finally resumed in 3065 when the Word of Blake, working in conjunction with the Free Worlds League, overcame the technical challenges and restarted manufacturing on Terra and Gibson. ComStar responded by establishing a production facility on Tukayyid, sharing output with the Second Star League and deploying Mk. XXIs to several Fury Teams. That factory operated for barely two years before the Word of Blake destroyed it in 3067.

During the Jihad, the Word of Blake supplied Nighthawk suits to Protectorate Militia infantry units, using the light power armor to bolster garrison forces while reserving heavier battle armor designs for frontline formations. ComStar, crippled by the loss of Tukayyid and the casualties suffered during Case White, took years to restart production at Arc-Royal MechWorks. Even after the facility came online, output remained limited, with priority going to personnel engaged in covert operations.

The Niops Association provided an unexpected additional source beginning around 3071, when engineers discovered Star League-era production records in their archives. Niops Project Workshop III began manufacturing both the Mk. XXI and the Mk. XXII, primarily to defend against belligerent Periphery neighbors. The decision proved wise when the Marian Hegemony's VI Legio Ripariensis raided Niops V that same year. The legionnaires were completely unprepared for power-armored infantry, and the Nighthawk-equipped defenders routed the invaders. Small numbers of captured Nighthawks from Word of Blake stockpiles have since appeared on the open arms market, finding buyers among mercenary commands, private security firms, and militia forces willing to acquire any available battle armor technology.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Krupp Armament Works`,primaryFactory:`Terra`},`The Mk. XXX is a mobility-focused evolution of the Nighthawk platform. While retaining the Standard Stealth armor and ninety-meter jump capability common to the Nighthawk family, the Mk. XXX features a significantly improved ground speed of thirty kilometers per hour, roughly three times the pace of the Mk. XXI. This enhanced overland mobility makes the suit more effective for extended patrol routes and rapid ground-level repositioning between jump points, allowing troopers to cover greater distances without consuming jump jet fuel reserves.

Like other Nighthawk variants, the suit carries no integral weapons, depending on armored gloves and standard infantry arms for any combat requirement. The Mk. XXX remains just as vulnerable to heavy weapons as its siblings, filling a useful niche for missions where sustained ground movement takes priority over the stop-and-leap pattern typical of jump-equipped power armor.`,{manufacturer:`Tvastar Enterprises`,primaryFactory:`Regulus`},`Tvastar Enterprises' first entry into the battle armor market, the Ogre was designed to supplement slower vehicle platoons and fill gaps in Regulan planetary militias. Introduced in 3091, this heavy Inner Sphere suit was built on Regulus during a period of increasing reliance on non-BattleMech forces across the former Free Worlds League. The Ogre's unusually wide frame and paired melee weapons give it an intimidating battlefield presence that belies its intended support role. Though ill-suited to anti-infantry work, the suit earned a fearsome, if exaggerated, combat reputation through its effectiveness against armored vehicles.`,`The Interdictor configuration replaces a portion of the standard Ogre's SRM ammunition with an ECM Suite and upgrades the suit's protection to Mimetic Armor. This electronic warfare package disrupts enemy targeting systems and communications, while the adaptive camouflage allows troopers to close on targets with reduced detection. The combination enables the Interdictor to maneuver into more favorable positions than its standard counterpart before engaging.

The trade-off for this stealth capability is a significantly reduced missile loadout, limiting the Interdictor's ability to sustain prolonged firefights. The suit retains its paired Heavy Battle Claws and ground-level mobility profile, making it best employed in ambush or screening roles where its electronic warfare suite can disrupt enemy coordination before close-range engagement.`,`Tvastar Enterprises developed the Ogre in the years following the Jihad, capitalizing on a growing market for non-BattleMech combat units across the former Free Worlds League. The suit performed far better than anticipated, and Tvastar soon secured export contracts with the Duchy of Andurien and the Lyran Commonwealth.

The Ogre's tank-shredding reputation traces to a 3095 engagement on the Oriente Protectorate world of Emris IV. The Tenth Regulan Hussars deployed their Ogre troopers during an exploratory raid that escalated into contact with Steel Guard forces. Three Ogre squads ambushed by Vedette tanks in a narrow defile used their suits' heavy claws to disable and tear open the immobilized vehicles. BattleROM footage of the aftermath gave birth to the myth that Ogres could literally rip tanks in half, and the ritual destruction of disabled vehicles became a tradition among Ogre operators.

A small number of Ogres eventually reached the Rasalhague Dominion through merchant channels, finding an unexpected second market among Clan warriors who valued the suit's physical presence on the battlefield.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],$v:[[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#0`,8],[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#1`,null]]},`The Ogre carries paired Heavy Battle Claws as its primary melee weapons, capable of inflicting serious damage to vehicle armor at close range. An SRM-2 launcher with thirteen volleys of ammunition provides the suit's ranged striking power, giving operators enough firepower for sustained engagements. Nearly a full ton of standard armor provides considerable protection, allowing the Ogre to absorb fire from all but the heaviest vehicle-mounted weapons.

At 1,500 kilograms, the Ogre is classified as a heavy battle armor suit. Its ground speed of 22 kilometers per hour and complete lack of jump capability limit tactical mobility, making the suit dependent on transport or supporting units to close with targets. Despite its imposing size, the suit lacks anti-personnel weapons, leaving it poorly matched against conventional infantry.`,[{tag:`is-srm-2`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`,shots:13}],`Produced by Luthien Armor Works for the Draconis Combine Mustered Soldiery, the Oni is a medium battlesuit that entered service in 3134 as a replacement for the aging Raiden. The one-ton humanoid design employs mixed Inner Sphere and Clan technology, incorporating advanced protective plating to improve operator survivability. Intended as a flexible line unit, the Oni fills multiple battlefield roles through interchangeable weapon loadouts rather than any single specialization. The suit's name translates from Japanese as "ogre," a designation its operators have found grimly appropriate given the design's imposing but graceless silhouette.`,`The Bearhunter configuration mounts a Bearhunter Superheavy Autocannon on the right-arm modular weapon mount, fed by twenty rounds of ammunition. This loadout punishes other battle armor and conventional infantry at short range, delivering fire that can shred lighter suits quickly. A left-arm Heavy Vibro Claw handles close-quarters fighting, while a basic manipulator on the weapon arm allows interaction with equipment and OmniMech handholds for mechanized transport.

Three hundred and fifty kilograms of Clan-specification fire-resistant armor absorbs punishment from both conventional weapons and incendiary attacks. An ECM suite disrupts enemy targeting, and extended life support permits prolonged deployment in hostile environments. Ground speed of thirty-two kilometers per hour is adequate but unremarkable, and the Oni lacks any jump capability, limiting tactical mobility in rough terrain.`,`The DCMS commissioned Luthien Armor Works to develop a Raiden replacement during the early 3130s. Engineers drew on data from evaluations of the Clan Nova Cat Thunderbird suit, adapting several design principles for Inner Sphere production. The Oni entered service in 3134, with manufacturing lines on both Luthien and Savinsville.

Reception within the DCMS was poor. The Combine's warrior culture prizes honor and prestige, and the Oni's unremarkable capabilities and ungainly appearance earned it few admirers. The Procurement Department reinforced the suit's low standing by assigning it to garrison commands and units out of favor. Supply difficulties compounded these problems, as the Department shipped weapon loadouts based on its own assessments rather than unit requests. Quartermasters across the New Samarkand and Dieron Military Districts developed an informal gray market for trading modular weapon packs among themselves.

The suit's first recorded action came during the Combine's push to reclaim former Dieron Military District holdings. Positions fielding the Oni showed measurably higher survivability rates among defending personnel, and within a year the suit had been distributed to every planetary garrison across both districts. Heavy protective plating proved its strongest selling point, keeping troopers alive long enough to shift opinions among pragmatic field commanders.`,{$k:[`tag`],$v:[[`quirk-fast-reload`],[`quirk-non-standard-parts`]]},{la:{manipulator:`heavy-battle-claw-vibro`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`basic`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`clan-bearhunter-superheavy-ac`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#2`]]},`The medium recoilless rifle configuration equips the Oni's modular weapon mount with a Medium Recoilless Rifle and twenty rounds of ammunition, providing a balanced engagement profile against both armored and soft targets. The rifle offers greater effective range than the autocannon variant while retaining reasonable stopping power. A Heavy Vibro Claw on the left arm gives troopers a melee option, and the right arm's basic manipulator supports mechanized operations aboard OmniMechs.

The suit's three hundred and fifty kilograms of Clan-produced fire-resistant armor is its strongest defensive feature, providing protection well above what most Inner Sphere medium suits carry. An integrated ECM suite and extended life support round out the electronics package. At thirty-two kilometers per hour with no jump jets, the Oni relies on ground mobility alone, making terrain selection critical for effective employment.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-medium-recoilless-rifle`,`ra`,`#2`]]},`The Narc configuration fits a Compact Narc Launcher with four missile packs onto the modular weapon mount, shifting the Oni's role from direct combatant to target designator. Narc beacons attached to enemy units guide friendly missile fire with improved accuracy, making this variant valuable in combined-arms operations despite its limited ammunition count. The left-arm Heavy Vibro Claw provides a backup melee capability, while the right-arm basic manipulator supports mechanized transport.

Three hundred and fifty kilograms of Clan fire-resistant armor keeps troopers protected during the dangerous approach runs required to place beacons at effective range. An ECM suite and extended life support complete the electronics fit. Ground speed of thirty-two kilometers per hour and the absence of jump jets make closing with targets a challenge, placing a premium on coordination with faster friendly units.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],$v:[[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#0`,null],[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#1`,null],[`is-compact-narc`,`ra`,`#2`,4]]},`The particle projector configuration arms the Oni with a Support PPC and fourteen power packs through its modular weapon mount, giving troopers an energy weapon capable of engaging targets at longer ranges than the ballistic alternatives. The Support PPC hits hard for a battle armor weapon, though its limited ammunition supply demands careful fire discipline. The left-arm Heavy Vibro Claw remains effective in close combat, and a right-arm basic manipulator allows mechanized transport on OmniMechs.

Clan-specification fire-resistant armor at three hundred and fifty kilograms provides the Oni's primary survivability advantage, shrugging off incendiary weapons and absorbing considerable punishment. The onboard ECM suite helps mask the trooper's electronic signature, while extended life support sustains operations in vacuum or contaminated atmospheres. Without jump jets, the suit's thirty-two kilometer per hour ground speed is its only mobility option.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-support-ppc`,`ra`,`#2`]]},`The Phalanx is a heavy battle armor suit developed by Irian Technologies for the Free Worlds League Military. Entering production in 3065, the design filled a gap in the FWLM’s battle armor roster for a heavier platform capable of delivering sustained ranged firepower. Weighing 1,500 kilograms, the suit trades jump capability for ground speed and mounting flexibility, allowing troopers to ride OmniMechs into combat while retaining the ability to handle infantry-scale weapons. Advanced stealth protection makes the suit considerably harder to detect and engage than most contemporary heavy designs, though this comes at the cost of reduced overall durability.`,`The Free Worlds League Military began development of a heavy battle armor suit in 3059, seeking a domestic counter to designs like the Kanazuchi and Gnome. Irian Technologies led the effort, but persistent technical failures stalled the program for over two years. Processor malfunctions and myomer musculature breakdowns proved particularly resistant to resolution. The FWLM eventually permitted Word of Blake technical teams to join the project, and the Blakist scientists resolved the remaining issues with striking efficiency.

Production commenced in 3065 at Irian Technologies’ facilities on Irian, with the Word of Blake receiving purchasing rights as payment for their assistance. Free Worlds Defense Industries on Gibson began manufacturing shortly afterward, producing both the original configuration and a dedicated Blakist variant. During the Jihad, the Word of Blake expanded its use of the suit as a heavy fire support complement to lighter Purifier and Nighthawk designs. Alys Rousset-Marik’s resistance movement also acquired significant numbers of the original configuration, using them to provide fire support against protectorate militias.

Post-Jihad production continued under new political oversight. A second Irian BattleMechs Unlimited facility on Washburn came online around 3078, and the design remained in active service through the Dark Age. A modernized configuration appeared around 3132, proving popular with garrison and patrol commands across multiple successor states.`,{la:{manipulator:`battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`armored-glove`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],[`is-heavy-mortar`,`body`,`#0`]],designs:JSON.parse(`{"450":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~2","deployment":"The DEST-specific Kage was issued exclusively to Draconis Elite Strike Team operatives operating in four-trooper squads. These teams handled the highest-priority infiltration and direct action missions within the DCMS, where every trooper carrying a laser ensured the squad maintained offensive capability regardless of individual casualties.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor (DEST)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~5","era":"clan-inv"},"451":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~2","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of DEST-variant Kage suits appeared among Clan Nova Cat forces following technology exchanges with the Draconis Combine. Nova Cat Elementals adapted the all-laser loadout to Point-level tactics, though the suit's reduced jump range compared to standard configurations limited its appeal for front-line Clan warriors.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor (DEST)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~5","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"452":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~2","deployment":"Word of Blake intelligence units captured DEST-variant Kage suits during operations against Combine special forces and organized them into six-trooper Level I formations. The uniform laser armament and stealth capability made these formations valuable for Blakist counter-intelligence operations targeting resistance networks on occupied worlds.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor (DEST)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~5","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"453":{"tro":{"production":"~6","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~7","deployment":"The Draconis Combine Admiralty issued space-configured Kage suits to four-trooper boarding squads assigned to WarShip and armed JumpShip crews. These teams trained for zero-gravity assault operations, using the suit's jump boosters and battle claws to breach hostile vessels during naval engagements.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor (Space)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~8","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~9","era":"civil-war"},"454":{"tro":{"production":"~6","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~7","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of space-variant Kage suits saw limited use among Clan naval forces allied with the Combine. The boarding configuration filled a niche for light assault teams that could infiltrate through access points too small for standard Elemental suits during shipboard operations.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor (Space)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~8","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~9","squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"455":{"tro":{"production":"~6","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~7","deployment":"Word of Blake naval forces organized captured space-configured Kage suits into six-trooper Level I boarding teams during the Jihad. Blakist commanders valued the suit's compact size and stealth for covert boarding actions against Combine naval assets and civilian JumpShips along contested supply routes.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor (Space)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~8","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~9","squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"456":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~10","deployment":"Vibro-claw Kage squads emerged from the fighting on Luthien in 3069, where four-trooper DCMS teams employed them against Blakist infantry and unarmored personnel in the close confines of urban combat. These squads were attached to frontline regiments engaged in building-to-building clearance operations where ranged weapons risked collateral damage.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor (Vibro-Claw)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~11","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~12","era":"jihad"},"457":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~10","deployment":"Clan Nova Cat organized vibro-claw Kage suits into five-trooper Points for close-combat operations. The melee-only configuration appealed to warriors who valued personal combat, though the complete lack of ranged weaponry limited the variant's tactical flexibility compared to standard Clan battle armor designs.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor (Vibro-Claw)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~11","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~12","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"458":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~10","deployment":"Word of Blake units captured vibro-claw Kage suits during the Jihad and organized them into six-trooper Level I formations for close-quarters security duties. The stealth-capable melee suits were deployed as silent perimeter teams around sensitive Blakist installations, where their lack of ranged weapons was offset by the need for covert elimination of intruders.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor (Vibro-Claw)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~11","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~12","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"459":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~13","deployment":"Clan Nova Cat Points operating flamer-equipped Kage suits deployed them in urban pacification roles following the alliance with the Combine. Five-trooper teams used the flamer to clear structures and deny cover to conventional infantry during security operations on jointly administered worlds.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~14","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"460":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~13","deployment":"Word of Blake militia units fielded salvaged flamer-equipped Kage suits in six-trooper Level I formations during the occupation of Combine border worlds. The stealth-capable suits were used for punitive raids against resistance cells, where the flamer's psychological effect supplemented its tactical utility.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~14","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"461":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~15","deployment":"Clan Nova Cat organized laser-armed Kage suits into five-trooper Points for reconnaissance screening. The energy weapon loadout aligned with Clan logistical preferences for ammunition-independent systems, and Nova Cat warriors adapted the suits for flanking operations within their combined-arms Stars.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~16","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"462":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~15","deployment":"ComStar's Com Guards acquired small numbers of laser-equipped Kage suits through diplomatic exchange with the Combine and organized them into six-trooper Level I formations. These units served in garrison and reconnaissance roles on worlds along the Combine-ComStar border prior to the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~16","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"463":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~17","deployment":"Clan Nova Cat Points employing machine gun-equipped Kage suits used them primarily in garrison duties and anti-bandit operations, where the automatic weapon's volume of fire proved more practical than energy weapons against dispersed conventional opponents on jointly held Combine worlds.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor [MG](Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~18","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"464":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~17","deployment":"Word of Blake forces organized captured machine gun-armed Kage suits into six-trooper Level I formations for base security operations. The combination of stealth and automatic weapons fire made these formations effective perimeter defense units on occupied Combine installations during the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor [MG](Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~18","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"465":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~19","deployment":"TAG-equipped Kage squads became the preferred forward observer element for DCMS artillery formations. Four-trooper teams infiltrated deep into enemy territory to designate targets for Arrow IV batteries, a role that exploited the Kage's stealth and jump range to reach and hold observation positions that conventional spotters could not access.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor [TAG](Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~20","apMounts":"~21","era":"clan-inv"},"466":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~19","deployment":"Clan Nova Cat organized TAG-carrying Kage suits into five-trooper Points to support combined-arms operations alongside Combine artillery assets. The designation capability proved valuable during joint exercises and garrison operations, where Nova Cat commanders integrated Combine indirect fire doctrine into their tactical planning.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor [TAG](Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~20","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"467":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~19","deployment":"ComStar forces equipped with TAG-carrying Kage suits organized them into six-trooper Level I formations for artillery coordination duties. The Com Guards used these teams to direct semi-guided munitions during defensive operations, pairing the Kage's stealth with the long reach of supporting fire batteries.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor [TAG](Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":5,"items":"~20","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"468":{"tro":{"production":"~6","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~22","deployment":"The experimental Kage C was developed at New Samarkand Metals and issued in limited numbers to four-trooper DEST squads for field evaluation. The mimetic armor and C3 networking capability were intended to enhance coordinated reconnaissance operations, though initial field trials drew criticism from operators over the complete absence of offensive armament.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor C(Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~23","era":"jihad"},"469":{"tro":{"production":"~6","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~22","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of Kage C suits were considered for joint operations with Clan Nova Cat forces, where the C3 system's ability to share targeting data across networked formations offered potential for coordinated strikes. The experimental nature of the design and reliance on salvaged Blakist technology limited wider adoption.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor C(Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~23","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"470":{"tro":{"production":"~6","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~22","deployment":"Six-trooper Level I formations of the Kage C were proposed for intelligence-gathering roles, where the mimetic armor's superior concealment and C3 networking would allow larger observation teams to coordinate surveillance across wider operational areas. Limited availability of the experimental suits restricted actual deployment to small-scale trials.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kage Light Battle Armor C(Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~23","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"471":{"tro":{"production":"~24","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~26","deployment":"The upgraded Kanazuchi entered DCMS service in four-trooper squads, gradually replacing aging standard-configuration suits in front-line urban defense units. The improved ranged firepower allowed squads to engage threats at greater distances, reducing the suit's reliance on close-quarters fighting. Garrison commands across the Combine's border districts received priority allocation as the Jihad intensified.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor (Upgrade) [Battle Claw](Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~28","armorPoints":15,"items":"~29","apMounts":"~30","era":"jihad"},"472":{"tro":{"production":"~24","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~26","deployment":"Word of Blake formations fielded six-trooper Level I detachments of upgraded Kanazuchi suits seized from Combine stockpiles during the Jihad. The enhanced particle cannon and missile loadout complemented Blakist defensive doctrine, and the suit's heavy armor made Level I formations effective at holding urban strongpoints during occupation operations.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor (Upgrade) [Battle Claw](Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~28","armorPoints":15,"items":"~29","apMounts":"~30","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"473":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~32","deployment":"DCMS formations organized their Kanazuchi troopers into standard four-trooper squads, typically attached to BattleMech companies operating in urban defense zones. Elite units such as the Otomo and the Ghost Regiments fielded dedicated Kanazuchi squads as building-clearing and perimeter security assets. During the retaking of Proserpina, the Iron Will battalion of the First Ghost employed two full platoons of Kanazuchi armor in urban combat, earning distinction through methodical clearance of key cities and transport hubs.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor [Battle Claw](Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~28","armorPoints":15,"items":"~33","apMounts":"~30","era":"clan-inv"},"474":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~32","deployment":"Clan forces that encountered the Kanazuchi during the Combine-Ghost Bear War occasionally captured intact examples. Organized into standard five-trooper Points, these captured suits saw limited use among garrison formations and second-line Clusters. The suit's lack of mobility and inability to ride OmniMech handholds clashed with standard Clan battle armor doctrine, restricting captured Kanazuchi Points to static defensive assignments where their heavy armor could be exploited without requiring rapid redeployment.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor [Battle Claw](Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~28","armorPoints":15,"items":"~33","apMounts":"~30","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"475":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~34","deployment":"DCMS engineer squads employed the drill-equipped Kanazuchi for urban demolition and fortification breaching operations. Four-trooper squads attached to combat engineering companies proved effective at clearing obstacles and creating entry points through reinforced structures, supporting assault infantry advancing through contested city blocks. The armored drilling teams operated in environments too dangerous for conventional engineering vehicles.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor [Industrial Drill](Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~35","armorPoints":15,"items":"~33","apMounts":"~30","era":"clan-inv"},"476":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~34","deployment":"Clan garrison formations occasionally pressed captured drill-equipped Kanazuchi suits into service for engineering tasks. Organized into five-trooper Points, these assets supplemented dedicated Clan engineering vehicles in base construction and fortification work on occupied Combine worlds, though Clan warriors viewed such assignments with little enthusiasm.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor [Industrial Drill](Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~35","armorPoints":15,"items":"~33","apMounts":"~30","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"477":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~34","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia formations used six-trooper Level I detachments of drill-equipped Kanazuchi suits during their occupation of Combine worlds. The engineering capability proved useful for demolishing defensive works and clearing urban rubble during pacification operations across the Dieron district.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor [Industrial Drill](Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~35","armorPoints":15,"items":"~33","apMounts":"~30","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"478":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~36","deployment":"DCMS logistics squads fielded the salvage-equipped Kanazuchi in four-trooper detachments to recover battlefield materiel in contested urban areas. These squads operated close behind front-line formations, extracting reusable components from disabled equipment while their heavy armor protection allowed them to work under sporadic fire without requiring dedicated security escorts.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor [Salvage Arm](Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~37","armorPoints":15,"items":"~33","apMounts":"~30","era":"clan-inv"},"479":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~36","deployment":"Clan garrison forces adapted captured salvage-arm Kanazuchi suits into five-trooper recovery Points. The heavily armored salvage platform offered better survivability than unarmored technician teams working in contested areas, and Clan commanders assigned the duty to lower-caste personnel or freeborn warriors as a support role.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor [Salvage Arm](Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~37","armorPoints":15,"items":"~33","apMounts":"~30","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"480":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~25","capabilities":"~36","deployment":"ComStar technical detachments organized six-trooper Level I salvage teams using acquired Kanazuchi suits. The larger formation size allowed systematic recovery sweeps across engagement areas, with each trooper working a designated sector while the formation's combined armor and defensive weapons discouraged opportunistic attacks on the recovery operation.","battleHistory":"~27"}},"name":"Kanazuchi Assault Battle Armor [Salvage Arm](Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~37","armorPoints":15,"items":"~33","apMounts":"~30","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"481":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~40","deployment":"The Free Rasalhague Republic’s KungsArmé fielded the micro grenade launcher and flamer configuration in four-trooper squads attached to front-line infantry regiments. The combination of explosive suppression and close-range thermal weapons made these squads effective in clearing operations alongside conventional infantry, particularly during urban engagements against Word of Blake forces on Republic worlds.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [MicroGL/Flamer](Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~42","era":"jihad"},"482":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~40","deployment":"Clan Ghost Bear adopted limited numbers of Kobold suits organized into five-trooper Points, integrating them into garrison clusters where their stealth capabilities complemented heavier Elemental formations. Ghost Bear warriors used the grenade launcher and flamer loadout primarily for perimeter security and anti-infantry sweeps in support of larger combined-arms Stars.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [MicroGL/Flamer](Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~42","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"483":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~40","deployment":"ComStar’s Com Guards organized Kobold troopers into six-member Level Is distributed across Precentor Martial Davion’s reorganized armies. The micro grenade launcher and flamer configuration saw action with elements of the First and Fourth Armies, where Level Is operated as screening forces for heavier formations during the Jihad-era campaigns on Coventry and Hesperus II.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [MicroGL/Flamer](Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~42","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"484":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~43","deployment":"Republic infantry commanders favored the micro grenade launcher and small pulse laser combination for extended patrol operations, where the pulse laser’s accuracy and lack of ammunition requirements reduced logistical strain on forward-deployed four-trooper squads. KungsArmé formations equipped with this loadout frequently operated in mixed battle armor platoons alongside heavier suit types.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [MicroGL/SPL](Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~44","era":"jihad"},"485":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~43","deployment":"Ghost Bear garrison clusters that received Kobold suits organized them into standard five-trooper Points. The micro grenade launcher and pulse laser configuration provided Clan warriors with a self-sufficient light scouting element that could sustain operations without the ammunition constraints associated with ballistic loadouts, though most Bears viewed the suits as secondary to their Elemental inventory.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [MicroGL/SPL](Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~44","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"486":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~43","deployment":"Com Guard Level Is equipped with this configuration served primarily in rear-area security and garrison roles across the six armies. The pulse laser’s energy-based operation appealed to ComStar logisticians managing dispersed Level I detachments that could not always rely on regular ammunition resupply from increasingly strained supply chains.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [MicroGL/SPL](Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~44","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"487":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~45","deployment":"The TAG-equipped Kobold configuration proved particularly valuable to Republic forces working in coordination with Arrow IV artillery batteries and semi-guided missile carriers. Four-trooper squads operating in this spotter role advanced ahead of armored formations, designating priority targets from concealed positions while their micro grenade launchers provided self-defense against probing infantry.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [MicroGL/TAG](Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~46","era":"jihad"},"488":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~45","deployment":"Ghost Bear Points equipped with the TAG loadout filled a forward observation role within garrison clusters, painting targets for Clan artillery assets. The five-trooper formation provided redundancy, ensuring the loss of one or two troopers would not eliminate the Point’s ability to maintain target designations during sustained engagements.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [MicroGL/TAG](Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~46","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"489":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~45","deployment":"ComStar deployed TAG-equipped Kobolds as dedicated spotters integrated into combined-arms Level IIs alongside light OmniMechs. Elements of the First and Fourth Armies on Coventry, Hesperus II, and Galatea grouped these six-trooper Level Is with Arrow IV-equipped vehicles and semi-guided LRM carriers, creating formations purpose-built for indirect fire coordination.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [MicroGL/TAG](Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~46","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"490":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~47","deployment":"The small laser and flamer combination saw service with Republic four-trooper squads assigned to urban defense garrisons, where the all-energy weapon loadout eliminated ammunition concerns during protracted engagements. KungsArmé commanders appreciated the configuration’s reliability for building-to-building operations against Blakist ground forces during the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [SL/Flamer](Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~48","era":"jihad"},"491":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~47","deployment":"Ghost Bear Points using the small laser and flamer configuration typically served in second-line garrison formations, where their energy-based weapons required minimal logistical support. Bear warriors used these Points for area-denial missions around supply depots and communication nodes, leveraging the flamer’s psychological impact and the laser’s consistent output.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [SL/Flamer](Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~48","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"492":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~47","deployment":"Com Guard Level Is carrying this all-energy configuration were distributed among rear-echelon security detachments, where their independence from ammunition supply lines made them attractive for static defense assignments. These six-trooper teams could sustain combat operations for extended periods without resupply, a critical advantage as ComStar’s logistical networks deteriorated during the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [SL/Flamer](Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~48","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"493":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~49","deployment":"Republic squads carrying small lasers with TAG designation equipment served in mixed reconnaissance and fire-support roles, combining credible self-defense capability with forward observation duties. Four-trooper teams operating ahead of KungsArmé armored columns could engage light opposition while simultaneously providing targeting data for indirect fire assets.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [SL/TAG](Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~50","era":"jihad"},"494":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~49","deployment":"Ghost Bear Points with the small laser and TAG loadout balanced independent combat capability with artillery spotting, making them useful in the screening role that Clan doctrine assigned to lighter battle armor types. These Points operated at the edges of Ghost Bear defensive positions, engaging light probes while coordinating fire from heavier assets behind them.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [SL/TAG](Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~50","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"495":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~49","deployment":"ComStar’s Com Guards valued this configuration for its dual capability as both a combat and observation platform. Six-trooper Level Is equipped with small lasers and TAG were attached to combined-arms Level IIs, where they could contribute direct fire while maintaining their primary mission of designating targets for artillery and guided munitions.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor [SL/TAG](Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":5,"items":"~50","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"496":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~51","capabilities":"~52","deployment":"Rasalhague Dominion garrison units organized along Inner Sphere infantry conventions employed the Kobold IIC in four-trooper squads for aerial scouting missions. Former FRR infantry traditions persisted in some Dominion formations, and these squads provided a VTOL-capable reconnaissance element that complemented heavier ground-based battle armor in mixed infantry platoons.","battleHistory":"~53"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor IIC (Sqd4)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"light","motive":"vtol","motiveMP":6,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":5,"items":"~54","era":"early-rep"},"497":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~51","capabilities":"~52","deployment":"Standard Clan Points of Kobold IIC troopers served within the Rasalhague Dominion touman as dedicated aerial scouts and forward observers. Five-trooper Points exploited the suit’s VTOL capability and improved stealth to range ahead of advancing clusters, providing targeting data and battlefield intelligence that heavier Elemental Points could not gather without sacrificing concealment.","battleHistory":"~53"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor IIC (Sqd5)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"light","motive":"vtol","motiveMP":6,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":5,"items":"~54","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"498":{"tro":{"production":"~38","text":{"overview":"~51","capabilities":"~52","deployment":"Kobold IIC suits organized into six-trooper formations appeared in Dominion garrison units that retained ComStar-derived organizational structures. These larger teams leveraged the suit’s VTOL mobility and ECM suite for extended area surveillance missions, maintaining persistent overwatch of critical installations and supply corridors where the additional troopers provided valuable redundancy for long-duration operations.","battleHistory":"~53"}},"name":"Kobold Battle Armor IIC (Sqd6)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"light","motive":"vtol","motiveMP":6,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":5,"items":"~54","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"499":{"tro":{"production":"~55","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~56","deployment":"The Free Rasalhague Republic provided test troopers from the Second Kavalleri for the X-C3 evaluation program, organizing them into four-member squads consistent with standard KungsArmé doctrine. Republic participation in the trials reflected the ongoing cooperative relationship between ComStar and the FRR that had originally produced the Kobold design.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"quirks":"~57","name":"Kobold Battle Armor X-C3(Sqd4)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":4,"items":"~58","era":"jihad"},"500":{"tro":{"production":"~55","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~56","deployment":"Clan Ghost Bear contributed a small number of warriors to the X-C3 test program, organizing them into five-trooper Points. Bear participation was limited and cautious, reflecting the Clan’s general skepticism toward Inner Sphere electronics, though the C3i system’s potential for coordinated fire drew enough interest to justify a trial commitment.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"quirks":"~57","name":"Kobold Battle Armor X-C3(Sqd5)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":4,"items":"~58","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"501":{"tro":{"production":"~55","text":{"overview":"~39","capabilities":"~56","deployment":"ComStar served as the primary operator and developer of the X-C3 variant, fielding six-trooper Level Is as the core test formation. Testing with augmented units proved effective in urban environments and close-quarters terrain, where the C3i network’s shared targeting data gave even these lightly armed troopers a force-multiplying effect that exceeded initial projections.","battleHistory":"~41"}},"quirks":"~57","name":"Kobold Battle Armor X-C3(Sqd6)","weightClass":"light","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":4,"items":"~58","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"502":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~61","deployment":"Free Worlds League and Oriente Province forces organized Kopis squads into four-trooper formations, the standard Inner Sphere battle armor squad structure. Squads were typically deployed in pairs as urban defense anchors, exploiting the suit's heavy armament and endurance to hold chokepoints against numerically superior forces. The Twelfth Atrean Dragoons fielded some of the earliest Kopis squads during the final stages of the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor 'The Dark Alley'(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~64","apMounts":"~65","era":"jihad"},"503":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~61","deployment":"Clan forces that acquired Kopis suits through battlefield salvage or trade organized them into five-trooper Points following standard Clan doctrine. The suit's slow ground speed and Inner Sphere origin limited its appeal among frontline Clan units, though some garrison clusters found the design's heavy armament and sustained firepower useful for defensive operations where mobility was less critical.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor 'The Dark Alley'(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~64","apMounts":"~65","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"504":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~61","deployment":"Republic of the Sphere forces adopted six-trooper Level I formations for their Kopis suits, following the ComStar-derived organizational model. Republic commanders paired Kopis Level I formations with Federated Suns Grenadier squads to create mixed battle armor teams assigned to planetary militia garrisons, maximizing the concentrated firepower of the heavily armed suit.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor 'The Dark Alley'(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~64","apMounts":"~65","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"505":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~66","deployment":"The AI Mk II became the standard anti-personnel Kopis variant for Oriente Province forces, with four-trooper squads replacing the original Anti-Infantry configuration across garrison formations. The ballistic-heavy loadout and improved sensors made these squads effective at security patrols and perimeter defense, roles where the suit's low speed was less of a disadvantage.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (AI Mk II)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~67","era":"early-rep"},"506":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~66","deployment":"Clan Sea Fox traders occasionally brokered Mk II suits to Clan clients, who organized them into five-trooper Points. The configuration's reliance on ballistic ammunition rather than energy weapons made resupply more complex for Clan logistics chains, limiting adoption to garrison and second-line formations where ammunition availability was less constrained.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (AI Mk II)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~67","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"507":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~66","deployment":"Republic Armed Forces units organized Mk II Kopis suits into six-trooper Level I formations, continuing the ComStar-derived organizational model. The improved sensors on the Mk II enhanced the effectiveness of these larger formations by giving more troopers access to better battlefield awareness, allowing Level I commanders to coordinate suppressive fire across wider engagement zones.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (AI Mk II)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~67","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"508":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~68","deployment":"Oriente Province forces adopted the reactive-armored Mk IIr as their primary Kopis anti-personnel variant, fielding four-trooper squads across garrison and militia formations. The reactive armor's enhanced survivability against missile fire proved especially valuable during border skirmishes where opposing forces frequently employed indirect fire support.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (AI Mk IIr)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~69","era":"late-rep"},"509":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~68","deployment":"Five-trooper Kopis Mk IIr Points appeared primarily among Clan forces operating in former Republic space, where battlefield salvage provided a steady supply of the reactive-armored suits. The enhanced armor protection made the slow-moving Kopis more viable in open-field engagements where Clan warriors would otherwise dismiss the design's limited mobility.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (AI Mk IIr)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~69","squadSize":5,"era":"late-rep"},"510":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~68","deployment":"Republic and successor-state forces organized Mk IIr suits into six-trooper Level I formations, where the reactive armor's protection multiplied across the larger squad size to create formations with exceptional durability against ballistic and missile threats. These formations were frequently assigned to garrison duty on contested border worlds.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (AI Mk IIr)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~69","squadSize":6,"era":"late-rep"},"511":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~70","deployment":"Inner Sphere forces deployed four-trooper Anti-Infantry squads specifically for urban clearance operations during the Liberation of Terra. These squads worked in tight coordination, with troopers alternating flamer and pulse laser fire to systematically clear buildings and fortifications held by Word of Blake infantry.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Anti-Infantry)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~71","apMounts":"~65","era":"jihad"},"512":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~70","deployment":"Clan warriors who captured Anti-Infantry Kopis suits organized them into five-trooper Points. The configuration's close-quarters focus limited its tactical utility for Clan forces accustomed to ranged engagement doctrines, though some garrison Points employed the suits for facility defense and boarding actions where the flamer proved effective in confined spaces.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Anti-Infantry)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~71","apMounts":"~65","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"513":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~70","deployment":"Coalition forces at the Liberation of Terra organized some Anti-Infantry Kopis suits into six-trooper Level I formations, particularly among units with ComStar organizational heritage. The larger squad size proved advantageous in building-by-building fighting, where additional troopers provided better coverage of multiple entry points during clearance operations.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Anti-Infantry)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~71","apMounts":"~65","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"514":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~72","deployment":"Four-trooper Mortar squads served as organic indirect fire support for Oriente Province infantry formations. Commanders positioned these squads behind cover or on elevated terrain, where they could deliver heavy mortar rounds onto enemy positions while relying on reactive armor to survive counter-battery fire.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Mortar)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~73","apMounts":"~65","era":"late-rep"},"515":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~72","deployment":"Clan forces that acquired Mortar-variant Kopis suits organized them into five-trooper Points, though the configuration's indirect fire role was an unusual fit for Clan battle doctrine. Some garrison clusters employed Mortar Points as area denial assets, using sustained mortar fire to channel enemy advances into predetermined engagement zones.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Mortar)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~73","apMounts":"~65","squadSize":5,"era":"late-rep"},"516":{"tro":{"production":"~59","text":{"overview":"~60","capabilities":"~72","deployment":"Six-trooper Mortar Level I formations provided concentrated indirect fire support for Republic and successor-state combined arms formations. The larger squad size allowed these formations to sustain longer barrages, with troopers cycling fire to maintain continuous coverage of target areas during extended engagements.","battleHistory":"~62"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Mortar)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~63","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~73","apMounts":"~65","squadSize":6,"era":"late-rep"},"517":{"tro":{"production":"~74","text":{"overview":"~75","capabilities":"~76","deployment":"Federated Suns emergency response teams organized Krise operators into four-member squads, the standard Inner Sphere infantry formation size. These squads deployed across the Suns in civilian disaster relief roles, responding to building collapses, industrial accidents, and natural catastrophes. The suit's ease of operation allowed rapid training of emergency personnel who lacked formal powered armor experience.","battleHistory":"~77"}},"quirks":"~78","name":"Krise PA(L) (Sqd4)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~79","armorPoints":1,"items":"~80","era":"civil-war"},"518":{"tro":{"production":"~74","text":{"overview":"~75","capabilities":"~76","deployment":"Clan organizations that obtained Krise exoskeletons organized them into five-member Points consistent with standard Clan doctrine. The suit's simple controls and civilian focus made it accessible even to lower-caste operators, and Points of Krise-equipped personnel supplemented dedicated Clan rescue assets during large-scale emergencies on occupied worlds.","battleHistory":"~77"}},"quirks":"~78","name":"Krise PA(L) (Sqd5)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~79","armorPoints":1,"items":"~80","squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"519":{"tro":{"production":"~74","text":{"overview":"~75","capabilities":"~76","deployment":"ComStar and Word of Blake formations fielded the Krise in six-member Level I teams for disaster response and humanitarian operations. The larger team size provided additional manpower for complex rescue scenarios, and the suit's extended life support capability proved particularly valuable during prolonged operations in contaminated environments.","battleHistory":"~77"}},"quirks":"~78","name":"Krise PA(L) (Sqd6)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~79","armorPoints":1,"items":"~80","squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"520":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~83","deployment":"Free Worlds League Military squads of four troopers formed the backbone of Leonidas deployment in the David configuration. The light gauss rifle's range complemented standard Inner Sphere infantry tactics, allowing squads to engage from prepared positions during ambush operations. Export customers including the Capellan Confederation and various mercenary commands adopted the same four-trooper organization, integrating David-equipped squads into their reconnaissance and screening formations.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (David)(Sqd4)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~86","apMounts":"~21","era":"early-rep"},"521":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~83","deployment":"Clan-affiliated formations occasionally organized captured or traded Leonidas suits into five-trooper Points following standard Clan battle armor doctrine. The David configuration suited Point-level operations where concentrated ranged firepower from five troopers could suppress a target zone before heavier assets moved in. These Points were uncommon given the suit's Inner Sphere origins but appeared among mixed-technology Clan garrison forces during the late Dark Age.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (David)(Sqd5)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~86","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"522":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~83","deployment":"Organizations employing six-trooper Level I formations fielded the David configuration in dedicated fire support sections. The additional troopers over a standard Inner Sphere squad provided greater sustained firepower, extending the unit's ability to maintain suppressive fire during prolonged engagements. Republic-aligned and former ComStar-influenced military structures occasionally adopted this formation size for their battle armor contingents.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (David)(Sqd6)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~86","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"523":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~87","deployment":"The Firedrake configuration saw widespread use among Free Worlds League four-trooper squads assigned to counterinsurgency and internal security missions. The needler's effectiveness against lightly armored targets made it a preferred loadout for squads tasked with suppressing guerrilla activity in contested provinces. Mercenary commands and Capellan Confederation units that purchased the Leonidas through export channels often specified this configuration for urban security contracts.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (FireDrake)(Sqd4)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~88","apMounts":"~21","era":"early-rep"},"524":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~87","deployment":"Clan garrison forces that acquired Leonidas suits occasionally fielded the Firedrake configuration in five-trooper Points for anti-infantry screening duties. The needler loadout aligned with the Clan practice of assigning lighter battle armor to garrison clusters tasked with maintaining order on occupied worlds. These Points operated in support of heavier Clan battle armor formations rather than as independent combat elements.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (FireDrake)(Sqd5)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~88","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"525":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~87","deployment":"Six-trooper Level I formations using the Firedrake configuration served in security and policing roles within organizations structured around ComStar military doctrine. The larger formation size provided greater coverage during sweep operations, with troopers able to establish blocking positions across wider frontages. The suppressive capability of the needler made these Level I units effective in crowd control and low-intensity conflict scenarios.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (FireDrake)(Sqd6)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~88","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"526":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~89","deployment":"Free Worlds League garrison commands organized machine gun-equipped Leonidas squads into four-trooper teams for urban patrol and counterinsurgency duties. The standard Inner Sphere squad structure allowed integration with conventional infantry platoons, with battle armor squads providing the close-assault capability that unarmored troops lacked in built-up areas. Export customers in the Periphery found this configuration particularly useful for pacification operations.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (MG)(Sqd4)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~90","apMounts":"~21","era":"early-rep"},"527":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~89","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of machine gun-configured Leonidas suits appeared among Clan garrison forces that incorporated Inner Sphere salvage into their toumans. The Point structure concentrated anti-infantry firepower, making these formations effective for perimeter security and area denial. Clan commanders typically relegated captured Inner Sphere battle armor to second-line garrison clusters rather than frontline combat Stars.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (MG)(Sqd5)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~90","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"528":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~89","deployment":"Six-trooper Level I formations equipped with the machine gun configuration provided enhanced area suppression capability for organizations that adopted ComStar-derived military structures. The larger formation size increased the unit's ability to cover multiple approach routes simultaneously during defensive operations. These Level I units found regular employment in facility defense and convoy escort roles, where sustained firepower and stealth capabilities allowed them to establish overlapping fields of fire around protected assets.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (MG)(Sqd6)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~90","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"529":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~91","deployment":"Inner Sphere reconnaissance commands organized Sensor-equipped Leonidas suits into four-trooper squads for deep patrol and surveillance missions. The combination of improved sensors, stealth armor, and electronic countermeasures created a capable intelligence-gathering team. Free Worlds League border forces employed these squads along contested frontiers, where early detection of approaching hostile forces provided critical warning time for defensive preparations.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (Sensor)(Sqd4)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~92","apMounts":"~21","era":"early-rep"},"530":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~91","deployment":"Clan garrison forces occasionally organized Sensor-configured Leonidas suits into five-trooper Points for perimeter surveillance and early warning duties. The Point structure allowed continuous sensor coverage through staggered trooper deployment, maintaining detection capability around the clock. These Points supplemented heavier Clan reconnaissance assets on garrison worlds where dedicated scout platforms were unavailable or assigned to frontline duties.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (Sensor)(Sqd5)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~92","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"531":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~91","deployment":"Six-trooper Level I formations in the Sensor configuration provided enhanced surveillance capability for organizations built on ComStar military traditions. The additional troopers allowed commanders to deploy paired sensor teams with overlapping detection zones, creating a more comprehensive early warning network. These formations proved effective in monitoring wide perimeters around critical installations and supply depots.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (Sensor)(Sqd6)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~92","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"532":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~93","deployment":"Free Worlds League artillery commands valued TAG-equipped Leonidas squads as forward observation teams. Four-trooper squads infiltrated ahead of advancing formations to designate targets for supporting batteries, with the suit's stealth systems allowing them to operate deep in hostile territory. The Duchy of Oriente and later the reformed League prioritized this configuration for combined-arms operations where precision indirect fire coordination was essential.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (TAG)(Sqd4)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~94","apMounts":"~21","era":"early-rep"},"533":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~93","deployment":"Five-trooper Points equipped with the TAG configuration provided Clan-affiliated forces with forward observation capability tailored to standard Clan battle armor organization. The additional trooper compared to Inner Sphere squads allowed Points to maintain continuous target designation even if individual troopers were disabled. These Points typically operated in concert with Arrow IV-equipped OmniMechs or artillery vehicles organic to the garrison cluster.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (TAG)(Sqd5)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~94","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"534":{"tro":{"production":"~81","text":{"overview":"~82","capabilities":"~93","deployment":"Six-trooper Level I formations in the TAG configuration served as dedicated artillery spotting sections within organizations using ComStar-derived force structures. The larger formation allowed for paired observation teams within a single Level I, providing redundancy and wider area coverage for target designation. These units coordinated with fire support elements to deliver precise indirect fire against high-value targets.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Leonidas Battle Armor (TAG)(Sqd6)","groundMP":3,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":6,"items":"~94","apMounts":"~21","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"535":{"tro":{"production":"~95","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~97","deployment":"The experimental \\"Hacked\\" Longinus equipped with a David light Gauss rifle underwent limited trials with Regulan four-trooper squads. These squads tested the C³i relay concept in conventional combined-arms exercises, feeding targeting data to heavier networked units from concealed positions. The Principality of Regulus shelved the program after trials confirmed the armor and weapons trade-offs were too severe for sustained combat operations.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor 'Hacked' [David](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":6,"items":"~100","apMounts":"~101","era":"jihad"},"536":{"tro":{"production":"~95","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~97","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of David-armed \\"Hacked\\" Longinus suits represented a theoretical organizational option that saw no confirmed operational deployment. The Regulan trials focused on four-trooper squads, and the project was cancelled before broader adoption could occur.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor 'Hacked' [David](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":6,"items":"~100","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"537":{"tro":{"production":"~95","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~97","deployment":"Six-trooper Level I formations of David-equipped \\"Hacked\\" Longinus suits were never fielded operationally. The experimental C³i project remained confined to Regulan trials using standard Inner Sphere squad organization before cancellation, and no ComStar or Word of Blake formations adopted the design.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor 'Hacked' [David](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":6,"items":"~100","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"538":{"tro":{"production":"~95","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~102","deployment":"Machine gun-armed \\"Hacked\\" Longinus suits were tested alongside the David variant in Regulan four-trooper squads. The MG configuration offered suppressive fire capability for squads acting as forward sensor nodes, though the reduced armor left troopers dangerously exposed when operating within machine gun range. Trial deployments confirmed the design's limitations, and all test units were reportedly withdrawn from service.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor 'Hacked' [MG](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":6,"items":"~103","apMounts":"~101","era":"jihad"},"539":{"tro":{"production":"~95","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~102","deployment":"No confirmed operational deployment of MG-armed \\"Hacked\\" Longinus suits occurred in five-trooper Point organization. The Regulan experimental program did not extend beyond initial squad-level trials before the Principality shelved the project entirely.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor 'Hacked' [MG](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":6,"items":"~103","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"540":{"tro":{"production":"~95","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~102","deployment":"Six-trooper Level I formations of MG-equipped \\"Hacked\\" Longinus suits were never organized. The experimental design remained a Regulan prototype program that concluded without broader adoption by any faction using Level I battle armor formations.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor 'Hacked' [MG](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":6,"items":"~103","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"541":{"tro":{"production":"~104","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~105","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of Magnetic Longinus suits appeared in garrison formations that combined Clan-style organization with the practical need to transport battle armor on conventional platforms. The magnetic clamps removed the OmniMech dependency that normally constrained battle armor deployment, allowing Points to attach to any available BattleMech for rapid battlefield repositioning.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor (Magnetic)(Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~106","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"542":{"tro":{"production":"~104","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~105","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia Level I sections equipped with the Magnetic variant leveraged the larger formation size to overwhelm dismounting defenses. Six troopers riding into battle on the hulls of supporting BattleMechs could detach simultaneously and swarm a target from multiple angles, a tactic that Blakist commanders employed to devastating effect during Protectorate security operations.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor (Magnetic)(Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~106","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"543":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~108","deployment":"When the \\"David\\" light Gauss rifle became available in 3063, FWLM commanders quickly adopted the configuration for squads tasked with open-terrain operations. Four-trooper squads equipped with the ranged weapon could engage targets at distances where other Longinus loadouts would need to close, giving FWLM battle armor a standoff capability it previously lacked. David-armed squads saw particular use in the rolling plains and open farmland common to many League border worlds.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [David](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~109","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~110","apMounts":"~101","era":"civil-war"},"544":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~108","deployment":"Five-trooper Points fielding the David light Gauss rifle configuration operated with forces accustomed to Clan organizational patterns. The ranged engagement capability suited the Clan preference for opening engagements at distance before closing to finish, and Points equipped with the David rifle could contribute meaningful firepower in the early phases of a skirmish without exposing themselves to return fire.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [David](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~109","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~110","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"545":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~108","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia Level I formations equipped with David-armed Longinus suits provided long-range fire support within Blakist garrison commands. The six troopers' combined light Gauss fire gave these sections a ranged punch that complemented heavier BattleMech and vehicle assets, allowing Militia commanders to extend their engagement envelope without committing armored units forward.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [David](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~110","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"546":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~111","deployment":"Free Worlds League Military squads equipped with flamer-armed Longinus suits frequently drew assignments in urban pacification and defensive clearing operations. Four-trooper squads proved well-suited for sweeping corridors and flushing entrenched infantry from hardened positions, where the flamer's area suppression compensated for its limited range. FWLM commanders often paired these squads with conventional infantry platoons to secure contested buildings floor by floor.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~112","apMounts":"~101","era":"clan-inv"},"547":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~111","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of flamer-equipped Longinus suits saw limited service with Clan-affiliated forces that acquired the design through battlefield salvage or trade. Wolf's Dragoons organized captured suits into standard Clan Points, employing them in garrison actions where their close-range firepower proved effective at area denial and perimeter clearing.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~112","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"548":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~111","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia formations organized Longinus suits into six-trooper Level I sections following the Captain-General's decision to supply the design. Flamer-armed Level I units excelled in the close-quarters fighting that characterized Blakist urban operations, and their larger formation size allowed Militia commanders to saturate a defensive position more quickly than the four-trooper FWLM squads they trained alongside.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~112","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"549":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~113","deployment":"The laser configuration became the preferred loadout for FWLM squads assigned to extended patrol and garrison duty, where the absence of ammunition dependency reduced logistical strain. Four-trooper squads operating along the League's extensive border regions could sustain operations for days without resupply, making the laser-armed Longinus a practical choice for frontier commands stretched thin across multiple systems.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~114","apMounts":"~101","era":"clan-inv"},"550":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~113","deployment":"Laser-equipped Longinus suits organized into five-trooper Points appeared among Clan-affiliated garrison forces that valued the loadout's endurance. Without the need for ammunition resupply, Points operating in remote postings or aboard WarShips could maintain readiness indefinitely, an advantage that suited the self-sufficient operational style of Clan second-line formations.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~114","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"551":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~113","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia Level I sections fielding laser-armed Longinus suits served as standing garrison elements on Protectorate worlds. The six-trooper formation provided enough firepower to discourage raiding parties and secure key installations without ammunition constraints, freeing Blakist supply chains to prioritize heavier frontline formations during the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~114","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"552":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~115","deployment":"Machine gun-armed Longinus squads filled a straightforward anti-infantry role within FWLM formations. Four-trooper squads assigned to rear-area security and convoy escort duties used the sustained automatic fire to suppress unarmored threats quickly, though ammunition limitations meant these squads required closer logistical support than energy-armed alternatives. The MG loadout remained common among garrison units that prioritized volume of fire over operational endurance.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [MG](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~116","apMounts":"~101","era":"clan-inv"},"553":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~115","deployment":"Five-trooper Points carrying machine gun-equipped Longinus suits served in secondary garrison roles with forces that adopted Clan organizational structure. The automatic fire capability gave these Points effective anti-infantry suppression, though the ammunition dependency made them less popular than laser-armed alternatives for extended independent operations.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [MG](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~116","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"554":{"tro":{"production":"~107","text":{"overview":"~96","capabilities":"~115","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia Level I formations armed with machine gun Longinus suits provided base defense and installation security across the Protectorate. The six-trooper formation's combined automatic fire could shred unarmored infantry formations at close range, making these sections effective deterrents against conventional ground assault. Militia commanders often positioned them at chokepoints and access corridors where the volume of fire compensated for limited range.","battleHistory":"~98"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [MG](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~99","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~116","apMounts":"~101","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"555":{"tro":{"production":"~117","text":{"overview":"~118","capabilities":"~119","deployment":"Marian Hegemony legions field the Marauder in four-trooper squads, the standard formation size for Inner Sphere battle armor. Marian commanders typically employ squads in a fire support role, using the suit's target designation capability to call in indirect fire while engaging at range. Taurian militia units and Periphery mercenary commands also operate the suit in this configuration.","battleHistory":"~120"}},"quirks":"~121","name":"Marauder Battle Armor (Sqd4)","weightClass":"heavy","armorPoints":9,"items":"~122","apMounts":"~65","era":"late-rep"},"556":{"tro":{"production":"~117","text":{"overview":"~118","capabilities":"~119","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of Marauders appear primarily among mercenary commands that have adopted Clan-style organizational structures. The additional trooper per formation increases the volume of fire a single Point can deliver, making the configuration attractive to commands that favor concentrated firepower over broader coverage.","battleHistory":"~120"}},"quirks":"~121","name":"Marauder Battle Armor (Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","armorPoints":9,"items":"~122","apMounts":"~65","squadSize":5,"era":"late-rep"},"557":{"tro":{"production":"~117","text":{"overview":"~118","capabilities":"~119","deployment":"Six-trooper formations following the Level I organizational model have appeared among commands with ties to former ComStar or Word of Blake military traditions. The larger formation size compensates somewhat for the suit's limited mobility by providing additional firepower per tactical element, though the logistical burden of maintaining six suits per formation can strain smaller units.","battleHistory":"~120"}},"quirks":"~121","name":"Marauder Battle Armor (Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","armorPoints":9,"items":"~122","apMounts":"~65","squadSize":6,"era":"late-rep"},"558":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~125","deployment":"Captured Nephilim suits equipped with tasers and LRM launchers were among the most closely studied Demon configurations. Four-trooper squads provided allied weapons researchers with insight into Blakist taser technology and the detachable weapon pack system, both of which influenced post-war battle armor development programs across multiple Inner Sphere states.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Capture Team)[HMG](Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~127","era":"jihad"},"559":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~125","deployment":"The capture team concept held no appeal for Clan warriors whose honor codes demanded the destruction of enemies in combat. Any Nephilim capture suits salvaged by Clan forces and organized into five-trooper Points would almost certainly have been stripped of their taser systems and reconfigured for direct combat roles.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Capture Team)[HMG](Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~127","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"560":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~125","deployment":"Capture team Nephilim troopers deployed in six-trooper Level I formations as specialized seizure units under direct Manei Domini authority. A full Level I could potentially disable multiple BattleMechs and vehicles in rapid succession, seizing both equipment and personnel for the Word's intelligence apparatus. The LRM launcher provided stand-off firepower during the approach phase, while the heavy machine gun sustained the engagement after the tasers were discharged or jettisoned.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Capture Team)[HMG](Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~127","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"561":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~128","deployment":"The Gauss Nephilim's precision ballistic weapons suite made it one of the more studied Demon configurations in allied evaluation programs. Four-trooper squads equipped with the triple-Gauss loadout provided valuable data on Blakist miniaturized Gauss technology, particularly the Magshot rifles that duplicated Federated Suns designs using Word of Blake manufacturing processes.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Gauss)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~129","era":"jihad"},"562":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~128","deployment":"Clan scientists would have found the Gauss Nephilim's weapons technology of considerable interest, as the miniaturized Gauss systems represented engineering approaches different from Clan designs. Any salvaged suits organized into five-trooper Points would likely have been directed to research facilities rather than combat formations.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Gauss)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~129","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"563":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~128","deployment":"Gauss-armed Nephilim troopers operated in six-trooper Level I formations as precision fire support. The all-ballistic weapons suite allowed sustained engagement without heat concerns, and the cybernetically enhanced Manei Domini operators could exploit the weapons' accuracy to devastating effect. This configuration was reportedly favored by the Domini elite for operations requiring precise, controlled application of force rather than area suppression.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Gauss)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~129","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"564":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~130","deployment":"The Narc variant's target designation capability made it tactically valuable to Inner Sphere forces familiar with Narc-guided munitions. Four-trooper squads of salvaged Narc Nephilim could integrate into existing combined-arms formations, tagging enemy units for allied missile batteries. This was one of the few Demon configurations with clear utility beyond specialized Manei Domini operations.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Narc)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~131","era":"jihad"},"565":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~130","deployment":"Narc-equipped Nephilim troopers operated in six-trooper Level I formations as forward target designators within larger Blakist combined-arms formations. The Narc beacon allowed these Level Is to tag enemy units for guided missile strikes from allied launchers, while the support PPC and Gauss weapons provided enough personal firepower to hold their own if the engagement closed to direct combat range. This made them effective bridge units between reconnaissance and fire support roles.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Narc)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~131","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"566":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~132","deployment":"Clan forces that engaged Nephilim troopers during the Jihad faced one of the most formidable battlesuits in existence. Any salvaged examples organized into five-trooper Points would have represented a significant intelligence prize, though the suit's two-ton mass and Inner Sphere technology base made integration into Clan formations impractical for sustained use.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Plasma)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~133","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"567":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~132","deployment":"Plasma-armed Nephilim troopers deployed in six-trooper Level I formations as heavy assault elements within Shadow Division task forces. Their mimetic armor and combined plasma-and-missile firepower allowed these Level Is to engage targets that would overwhelm lighter Demon suits. Allied intelligence confirmed the greatest concentrations in the Forty-fourth, Forty-seventh, Forty-ninth, and Fifty-second Shadow Divisions, with even those elite formations receiving limited numbers.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Plasma)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~133","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"568":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~134","deployment":"The Seeker configuration's enhanced sensor suite attracted significant intelligence interest from allied forces. Four-trooper squads assembled from captured examples contributed to post-war research into sensor-defeating countermeasures, as understanding the Seeker's detection capabilities helped develop tactics to counter similar threats in future conflicts.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Seeker) [MG](Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~135","era":"jihad"},"569":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~134","deployment":"No confirmed instances of Clan forces fielding Seeker-configured Nephilim suits in five-trooper Points have been documented. The hunter-killer role, while tactically useful, required the specialized sensor integration that was difficult to maintain without access to Blakist technical support infrastructure.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Seeker) [MG](Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~135","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"570":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~134","deployment":"Seeker Nephilim troopers deployed in six-trooper Level I formations as dedicated hunter-killer teams. Their improved sensors and mimetic armor created operators who could detect enemies while remaining nearly invisible, an advantage the Manei Domini exploited ruthlessly. These Level Is were typically assigned high-value targets: enemy command vehicles, scout lances, and forward observation posts that needed to be eliminated quickly and quietly.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Seeker) [MG](Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~135","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"571":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~136","deployment":"The support Nephilim's fixed weapons and expendable rocket launchers made it relatively straightforward for Inner Sphere operators to field in four-trooper squads. The small laser's indefinite ammunition supply reduced logistical demands, and the fixed mounting simplified maintenance compared to the modular-armed variants. Small numbers may have seen limited post-Jihad evaluation service.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Support)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~137","era":"jihad"},"572":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~136","deployment":"Clan forces showed no documented interest in the support-configured Nephilim. The fixed small laser and expendable rockets offered no capabilities that Clan battle armor designs did not already provide at lower mass, and the suit's two-ton weight precluded OmniMech transport, a capability Clan warriors considered essential.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Support)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~137","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"573":{"tro":{"production":"~123","text":{"overview":"~124","capabilities":"~136","deployment":"Support Nephilim troopers operated in six-trooper Level I formations as combined-arms fire elements. The opening rocket volley followed by sustained laser fire gave these Level Is a reliable engagement profile that demanded less coordination than the modular variants. Domini commanders valued this configuration for operations where communications might be disrupted, as each trooper carried a self-contained weapons package that functioned independently of network support.","battleHistory":"~126"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Support)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~137","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"574":{"tro":{"production":"~138","text":{"overview":"~139","capabilities":"~140","deployment":"Following the reintroduction of the Nighthawk in 3065, Inner Sphere military commands organized their operators into standard four-trooper squads consistent with conventional infantry doctrine. These squads typically served as dedicated reconnaissance and intelligence-gathering elements attached to larger formations. The compact squad size proved well-suited to the Nighthawk's covert operational profile, with four-trooper teams small enough to infiltrate sensitive areas while still providing mutual support during extraction under fire.","battleHistory":"~141"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXI(Sqd4)","weightClass":"pa-l","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"items":"~142","era":"star-league"},"575":{"tro":{"production":"~138","text":{"overview":"~139","capabilities":"~140","deployment":"The original SLDF deployment of Nighthawks through the Special Forces Command occasionally employed five-trooper teams for operations requiring additional personnel or redundancy. This organizational pattern carried forward when Nighthawk technology accompanied the Exodus fleet, though the Clans ultimately developed their own battle armor rather than continuing the Nighthawk line. In rare instances where salvaged or captured Nighthawks have been fielded by Clan-affiliated forces, the familiar five-trooper Point structure has been applied.","battleHistory":"~141"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXI(Sqd5)","weightClass":"pa-l","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"items":"~142","squadSize":5,"era":"star-league"},"576":{"tro":{"production":"~138","text":{"overview":"~139","capabilities":"~140","deployment":"ComStar and the Word of Blake deployed Nighthawks in six-trooper Level I formations consistent with Com Guard organizational doctrine. ComStar's Fury Teams were among the first units to receive newly manufactured Mk. XXIs from the Tukayyid production facility, operating at full Level I strength for covert operations. The Word of Blake similarly organized their Nighthawk-equipped personnel into Level I formations, particularly within Protectorate Militia units where the suits supplemented conventional infantry capabilities.","battleHistory":"~141"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXI(Sqd6)","weightClass":"pa-l","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"items":"~142","squadSize":6,"era":"star-league"},"577":{"tro":{"production":"~143","text":{"overview":"~139","capabilities":"~144","deployment":"The Mk. XXX's enhanced ground speed makes four-trooper squads particularly effective for wide-area scouting missions where rapid overland movement is critical. Inner Sphere commands that have acquired these rare suits typically assign them to reconnaissance squads operating ahead of main force elements, leveraging the thirty kilometer per hour ground speed to cover far more territory than standard Nighthawk teams.","battleHistory":"~141"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXX(Sqd4)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"era":"star-league"},"578":{"tro":{"production":"~143","text":{"overview":"~139","capabilities":"~144","deployment":"Five-trooper teams of the Mk. XXX exist primarily as an organizational option for forces using Clan-pattern formations. The variant's Star League origins and extreme rarity mean that Point-sized deployments have been exceedingly uncommon, limited to isolated instances where recovered suits were integrated into five-trooper teams following the operational practices of their parent command.","battleHistory":"~141"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXX(Sqd5)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"squadSize":5,"era":"star-league"},"579":{"tro":{"production":"~143","text":{"overview":"~139","capabilities":"~144","deployment":"The limited number of Mk. XXX suits in circulation has restricted six-trooper Level I formations to rare occasions where a command possessed sufficient quantity. The enhanced ground mobility would provide a Level I with improved area coverage compared to standard Nighthawk formations, though the scarcity of the variant has prevented widespread adoption in ComStar or Word of Blake service.","battleHistory":"~141"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXX(Sqd6)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"squadSize":6,"era":"star-league"},"580":{"tro":{"production":"~145","text":{"overview":"~146","capabilities":"~147","deployment":"Clan formations that acquired the Ogre organized Interdictor variants into five-trooper Points for specialized disruption roles. The ECM suite proved valuable in Clan combined-arms tactics, allowing Interdictor Points to screen advancing OmniMech formations while degrading enemy targeting and communications before heavier assets engaged.","battleHistory":"~148"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Interdictor)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~63","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":14,"items":"~149","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"581":{"tro":{"production":"~145","text":{"overview":"~146","capabilities":"~147","deployment":"The six-trooper Interdictor formation extends the electronic warfare footprint of each tactical element, providing broader ECM coverage across a wider frontage. Like the standard configuration, this larger squad size saw limited fielding.","battleHistory":"~148"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Interdictor)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~63","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":14,"items":"~149","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"582":{"tro":{"production":"~145","text":{"overview":"~146","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Regulan planetary militias organized their Ogre troopers into four-member squads following Inner Sphere infantry doctrine. These squads typically operated alongside vehicle platoons, leveraging the suit's anti-armor capability to compensate for gaps in militia BattleMech strength. The four-trooper formation became the standard organizational template for all Inner Sphere Ogre operators.","battleHistory":"~148"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Sqd4)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~63","armorPoints":14,"items":"~151","era":"early-rep"},"583":{"tro":{"production":"~145","text":{"overview":"~146","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Ogre suits acquired by the Rasalhague Dominion through Sea Fox merchants were organized into standard five-trooper Clan Points. Star Captain Idris DelVillar of the 283rd Battle Cluster employed his Ogre Points alongside Dasher OmniMechs, using the suit's bulk to exploit weak points in enemy formations. Initial Clan resistance to the Inner Sphere design was overcome after the Ogre proved itself in a Trial of Grievance.","battleHistory":"~148"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~63","armorPoints":14,"items":"~151","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"584":{"tro":{"production":"~145","text":{"overview":"~146","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"The six-trooper formation provides additional firepower per tactical element, making it an option for organizations that structure their battle armor units around larger squad sizes. This configuration saw limited adoption compared to the four and five-trooper formations.","battleHistory":"~148"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~63","armorPoints":14,"items":"~151","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"585":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~153","deployment":"DCMS militia commands field the Bearhunter configuration in standard four-trooper squads, where the superheavy autocannon's anti-infantry firepower suits garrison duty well. These squads typically anchor defensive positions, screening vulnerable gun crews and support personnel. The Bearhunter's short-range lethality against lighter battle armor makes it a common Procurement Department assignment for units tasked with rear-area security.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Bearhunter](Sqd4)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~157","era":"dark-ages"},"586":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~153","deployment":"Combine formations influenced by Clan Nova Cat organizational doctrine occasionally fielded Oni troopers in five-member Points. The additional trooper increased the Bearhunter variant's close-range volume of fire, making these Points effective in defensive screening actions. This formation pattern saw limited adoption before the Nova Cats' destruction, and few Combine units maintained the practice afterward.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Bearhunter](Sqd5)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~157","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"587":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~153","deployment":"Six-trooper formations of the Bearhunter configuration appeared in garrison commands that experimented with expanded squad structures modeled on ComStar's Level I organization. The larger formation concentrated more autocannon fire per tactical unit, though the increased logistical demands strained already difficult supply chains. Few commands sustained these oversized formations for extended periods.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Bearhunter](Sqd6)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~157","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"588":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~158","deployment":"The medium recoilless rifle variant proved the most popular assignment among DCMS militia squads, offering four-trooper teams a flexible engagement profile suited to the unpredictable nature of garrison operations. During the fighting on Misery in 3139, Ryuken-go infantry commanders assigned Oni squads to back up field gun teams during a pincer action against the Tenth Avalon Hussars, where the suits performed well in a defensive screening role.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [MRR](Sqd4)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~159","era":"dark-ages"},"589":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~158","deployment":"Five-trooper Points operating the recoilless rifle variant offered Clan-organized Combine formations a balanced direct-fire capability. The additional trooper provided useful redundancy for sustained engagements, as the medium recoilless rifle's ammunition consumption could leave individual troopers vulnerable during prolonged fighting. Combine units with Nova Cat heritage favored this configuration for its general-purpose reliability.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [MRR](Sqd5)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~159","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"590":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~158","deployment":"Expanded six-trooper formations carrying the medium recoilless rifle appeared in rear-echelon security units that adopted Level I organizational patterns. The larger squad provided sustained firepower over longer engagements, though quartermasters frequently noted the ammunition requirements of equipping six rifles per formation rather than the standard four.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [MRR](Sqd6)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~159","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"591":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~160","deployment":"The Compact Narc variant found a niche role among four-trooper militia squads operating alongside missile-equipped BattleMechs and vehicles. Effective employment demanded close coordination with supporting missile platforms, and commanders who invested in proper combined-arms training saw disproportionate returns from even a single Narc-equipped squad marking targets for friendly fire.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Narc](Sqd4)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~161","era":"dark-ages"},"592":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~160","deployment":"Five-trooper Points carrying the Compact Narc Launcher provided Clan-organized Combine formations with an organic target designation capability. The additional trooper improved survivability during the dangerous approach runs needed to place beacons, as the Point could suppress enemy positions while individual troopers closed to effective Narc range.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Narc](Sqd5)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~161","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"593":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~160","deployment":"Six-trooper Narc formations were uncommon, as the designator role rarely benefited from additional personnel beyond what was needed for basic tactical security. Commands that deployed oversized Narc squads typically divided them into fire teams, with one group providing security while the other maneuvered to place beacons on priority targets.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Narc](Sqd6)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~161","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"594":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~162","deployment":"Four-trooper squads armed with the Support PPC offered DCMS militia commanders a longer-range engagement option compared to the ballistic variants. These squads typically provided overwatch for defensive positions, engaging approaching threats before they closed to effective range. The PPC variant's limited ammunition supply meant careful fire discipline was essential, favoring experienced troopers over fresh recruits.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [PPC](Sqd4)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~163","era":"dark-ages"},"595":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~162","deployment":"Five-trooper Points equipped with the energy weapon configuration provided Clan-organized Combine formations with concentrated long-range firepower. The additional trooper helped offset the Support PPC's limited ammunition by distributing fire across more weapons. This formation saw occasional use among units that valued ranged engagement over close-quarters fighting.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [PPC](Sqd5)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~163","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"596":{"tro":{"production":"~31","text":{"overview":"~152","capabilities":"~162","deployment":"Six-trooper formations carrying the Support PPC were rare, as the energy weapon variant's higher training requirements made it difficult to fill oversized squads with qualified personnel. Commands that fielded these expanded formations typically deployed them in static defensive positions where the concentrated long-range fire justified the additional logistical overhead.","battleHistory":"~154"}},"quirks":"~155","name":"Oni Battle Armor [PPC](Sqd6)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~156","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~163","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"597":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Irian BattleMechs Unlimited|Irian BattleMechs Unlimited","primaryFactory":"Irian|Washburn"},"text":{"overview":"~164","capabilities":"The standard Phalanx configuration pairs a King David light gauss rifle with a detachable SRM-4 rack carrying an additional reload. The gauss rifle provides reliable long-range firepower, though its hitting power falls short of heavier man-portable weapons. The missile launcher compensates at closer ranges, and its detachable mounting allows troopers to jettison the empty pack when ammunition runs dry. A battle claw on the left arm enables OmniMech mounting, while an armored glove on the right permits handling of conventional infantry weapons and equipment requiring manual dexterity. This manipulator pairing sacrifices anti-BattleMech attack capability in favor of operational flexibility.\\n\\nGround speed reaches 22 km/h, adequate for keeping pace with most formations despite the suit’s lack of jump jets. Improved stealth armor reduces the sensor signature considerably, making the suit difficult to target at range. Protection is thin for the weight class, however, as concentrated energy fire can breach the armor in a single hit.","deployment":"Five-trooper formations operating the standard Phalanx configuration emerged among forces that adopted larger squad structures during the post-Jihad reorganization period. These teams leveraged the additional trooper to maintain sustained fire output over longer engagements, with the fifth member often carrying supplementary ammunition for the detachable missile packs.","battleHistory":"~165"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (A)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~166","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":{"$k":"~167","$v":[["is-srm-4","body","#0",1],["is-king-david-light-gauss-rifle","ra","#1",null]]},"squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"598":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Free Worlds Defence Industries|Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Gibson|Various"},"text":{"overview":"~164","capabilities":"The Blakist Phalanx variant replaces the King David light gauss rifle with a ’Mech Taser Rifle, giving troopers the ability to temporarily disable vehicles and BattleMechs through electrical disruption. The taser’s one-shot capability makes target selection critical, as a missed opportunity leaves the trooper reliant on the remaining SRM-4 rack for heavy firepower. Additional electronic hardening protects the suit’s internal systems from the feedback that taser discharge can produce, though the weapon still carries a risk of catastrophic overload.\\n\\nThe remaining equipment mirrors the standard configuration: a battle claw and armored glove for OmniMech mounting and infantry weapon handling, improved stealth armor for reduced targeting signature, and 22 km/h ground speed without jump capability. The taser’s specialized nature limits the configuration’s general combat utility, making it a precision tool for disabling high-value targets rather than a frontline combat platform.","deployment":"Word of Blake Level I formations made extensive use of the Phalanx B during the Jihad, employing six-trooper teams for targeted strikes against enemy command vehicles and key BattleMechs. The larger formation allowed for coordinated taser employment, with multiple troopers cycling fire to maximize the chances of a successful disabling shot against priority targets.","battleHistory":"~165"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (B)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~166","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":{"$k":"~167","$v":[["is-srm-4","body","#0",2],["is-taser","ra","#1",null]]},"squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"599":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Free Worlds Defence Industries|Irian BattleMechs Unlimited","primaryFactory":"Gibson|Washburn"},"text":{"overview":"~164","capabilities":"The Phalanx C configuration shifts the suit into an indirect fire support role, mounting a heavy mortar alongside a copy of the Magshot gauss rifle. The mortar allows troopers to deliver fire from behind cover and over obstacles, a capability few battle armor designs can match. The Magshot provides direct-fire backup with respectable accuracy at medium range.\\n\\nThis loadout transforms the suit from a frontline skirmisher into a fire support platform, though the mortar’s minimum range and relatively low rate of fire demand careful positioning. Mobility and defensive characteristics remain unchanged from the base chassis: 22 km/h ground movement, improved stealth armor for reduced sensor visibility, and the standard battle claw and armored glove combination for OmniMech transport and infantry weapon handling. The configuration lacks anti-BattleMech capability, relying instead on standoff distance and its stealth profile for survivability.","deployment":"Four-trooper squads fielding the Phalanx C provided organic indirect fire support to infantry formations that lacked conventional mortar or artillery assets. The heavy mortar’s ability to lob rounds over obstacles and into defilade positions gave squad-level commanders a capability previously reserved for dedicated support platoons.","battleHistory":"~165"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (C)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~166","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":{"$k":"~168","$v":["~169",["is-magshot-gauss-rifle","ra","#1"]]},"era":"jihad"}}`)};export{e as default};